WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:11.820
So I think this part of the course is always
a part I have difficulty with, because the

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.119
conceptual transformation is very, very large.

00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:22.130
Because so far we’ve been talking about,
I would say, biological and evolutionary realities,

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.040
in a sense.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.620
And we’ve been doing that in a relatively
standard manner.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:33.300
I mean, there are elements of Piagetian theory
that run through it, there are elements of

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.399
cybernetic theory, there are elements of neurobiology
and neuropsychology.

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:49.030
And none of it, I would say in some sense,
is radically outside the way these sorts of

00:00:49.030 --> 00:01:05.479
problems might be discussed in a typical scientific
or social scientific manner.

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:12.150
Except for maybe the fact that we’ve drawn
an explicit distinction between the world

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:19.050
as a place of things and the world as a place
of action.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.460
Now there are consequences to the idea that
the world is a place of action that start

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.210
to become more radical at this point.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:32.800
But drawing the relationship between what
I’ve already talked to you about and the

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:42.030
narrative domains that we’re going to explore
now, requires a… like a radical shift in

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.100
conceptual focus.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.160
And so, the way I’m going to handle that
is by hitting it from ten or eleven different

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:56.020
directions, and building up a pattern.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.420
You know, if you listen to a complex piece
of music, or at least this is my experience

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.770
with complex music, is sometimes, the first
time I hear it, I don’t really like it,

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.409
and I think I also don’t understand it.

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:08.250
I actually can’t hear it.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.080
You know, maybe a musical genius could hear
the whole thing instantly.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:18.410
You know, they say Franz Liszt could sight-read
anything on the piano first try.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.680
So, you know, some people are so intelligent
musically that it’s incomprehensible, and

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.080
I’m sure they hear things just fine the
first time they hear them.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.890
But then what happens for me is that if I
listen to it a couple more times or pieces

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.710
or chunks of it, they start to fall into place
that I can follow, and, you know, I can see

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.819
the beauty sort of shine through those.

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:42.051
And then eventually, the whole thing links
together, and then I can listen to it, especially

00:02:42.051 --> 00:02:46.420
if it’s a complex piece of music, many,
many, many times.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.290
And the more complex, and probably the better,
the piece of music, the more I can listen

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.290
to it.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:56.100
But if I listen to it enough, at some point
then I’ve had enough of it, and it’s all

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.600
a very strange phenomenon.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.830
Because one of the things you might ask yourself
is, what exactly are you learning while you’re

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:02.830
doing that?

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:10.020
You know, it seems like you want the music
to be just exactly the right amount of predictable

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.020
and unpredictable.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.319
If it’s too unpredictable then you can’t
understand it and if it’s too predictable

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:16.730
it’s boring, and so it’s just like a conversation
that way.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.180
It’s actually just like life as well!

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:24.160
You know, because you want things to be predictable
and stable, and you want things to be unpredictable

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.970
and interesting, and the degree to which you
want each of those is going to depend on the

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:35.790
time and the context and also on your own
intelligence and temperament.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:44.320
Now, I’m going to tell you a bunch of stories
and give you a number of pieces of information,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.470
and hopefully they’ll click together.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:52.030
Now I think the reason this information has
always been transmitted in story and image

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:57.489
form is because it’s very, very difficult
to transform it into articulated, like into

00:03:57.489 --> 00:03:58.849
fully articulated explanation.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:00.540
It isn’t really how it works.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.560
You know, we’re not really accustomed to
thinking about the idea that there are certain

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.680
forms of information (that are valid forms
of information) that can’t be transmitted

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:11.680
verbally.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.000
But of course, if you think about it, we all
understand this deeply.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.049
Well, I can give you a bunch of examples.

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:23.040
The first example is that when you speak,
not only do you speak in words and phrases

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.300
and sentences and paragraphs, but you also
speak melodically.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.570
And in fact, part of the reason that you can
understand melodies and that they make sense

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:39.139
to you is that what artists have done is separated
the melodic elements of speech from the semantic

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:41.730
element, and then played with the melodic
element.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:46.030
And the melodic element tends to carry a lot
of emotion and intent.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:51.810
And so, if you’re listening to someone speak,
you can tell when they’re being ironic,

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:55.320
you know, because they raise an eyebrow, and
their voice changes slightly, even though

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.510
they might be using, in fact they are using,
exactly the same words.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:03.630
And in fact someone who’s very good at being
witty or ironic will set the situation up

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:08.680
so that you can barely tell that they’re
joking, and the better they are at that, the

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:13.150
tinier the hint they give you that you can
still catch on to, the funnier the situation

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.220
is.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.140
So we know that information can be transmitted
through non-verbal channels.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.130
It even happens during speech.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:30.810
And then, of course, we understand that music
presents to us an intimation of meaning that’s

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.330
very difficult to fully grasp.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:40.530
And music for me has always been an ineffable
phenomenon in some sense because it carries

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:45.690
the intimation of meaning in a manner that
can’t be rationally dismissed, or that people

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.910
aren’t interested in rationally dismissing.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:55.620
Even punk rock nihilists listen to punk rock
nihilistic music, and they find that meaningful

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.810
even though they don’t believe in meaning!

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.169
And you know, in some sense they’re victims
of their own rationality, because they do

00:06:02.169 --> 00:06:04.740
believe in meaning (or else they wouldn’t
be listening to the music), but they can’t

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.729
understand how they could possibly believe
in it, so their rational mind has dispensed

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:10.729
with the idea.

00:06:10.729 --> 00:06:15.110
And sometimes people dispense with the idea
of meaning, for lots of reasons.

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:20.020
Partly because it’s inevitable, in some
sense, with sufficient rationality, but also

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:26.680
because it’s always useful to look for the
underbelly of things, because it also justifies

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:29.830
not bearing any responsibility for your life.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:36.610
And that’s fairly… that can be a very
desirable side-effect of the particular ideological

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:37.830
rational belief.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:42.980
And then there’s dance, of course, and dance
is even more ineffable than music, although

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:47.810
it’s often paired with music, you know,
and it grips people.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:53.100
If you see a remarkable dance performance,
well part of it is that you’re sort of thrilled

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.830
and excited about seeing just exactly what
the human form can do.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:03.730
Perhaps, partly, because you’re human and
when you see someone extending a certain ability

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:09.669
far beyond the norm then it expands your sense
of what a human being is capable of, so there’s

00:07:09.669 --> 00:07:11.750
that, there’s the pure skill element.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:17.400
And then there’s the novelty element, because
often if you watch a particularly good dancer

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.520
there’s things they do that you haven’t
seen before that you didn’t know anyone

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.450
could do and that’s pretty fun.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:27.919
And then often the dance is joined with the
music and the body is expressing what the

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:31.760
music means and even though you can’t tell
what the music means you can tell that the

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.530
dance is expressing it.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:39.380
See I think what happens there is that music
represents the patterns of being.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:43.139
It’s actually because people think of music
as a non-representational art, but I think

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:47.070
that’s wrong, I think it might be the most
representational art.

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:54.650
Being is made up of layers of patterns constantly
interacting, and hopefully in a relatively

00:07:54.650 --> 00:08:01.630
harmonious way, and music demonstrates that,
mimics it in some sense, abstractly.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:04.750
And then the dance is an abstract representation.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:09.030
People adapt to the patterns of being and
the dancer adapts to the pattern of music,

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:16.410
and so it’s symbolic, it’s an embodied
display of the place of the person in the

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:17.410
cosmos.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:21.380
And you could also make that case when you
see people dancing in pairs, because you could

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:28.960
dance alone or you could dance in pairs, and
that’s basically standard human adaptation.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:32.870
And music is interesting too because one of
the things you’ll notice is that there’s

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:35.409
always music in a movie.

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:36.409
Almost always.

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:39.719
There’s the odd movie that doesn’t have
it but it’s quite marked when it’s absent,

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:40.719
you know.

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:44.210
The movie feels a lot more cold and clinical,
although it can work.

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:49.360
But we accept the idea that music can be used
to fill in the missing context in a movie

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.400
without even ever questioning it, right?

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:57.020
Like, it’s very strange if you think about
it, that you go to a movie that is doing…

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:02.180
at least part of its function is to portray
reality in a realistic way, but there’s

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:04.260
a soundtrack playing in the background all
the time.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:11.200
You know it’s so useful and so appropriate
that you don’t notice how strange it is

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.730
that that’s okay.

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:20.510
So, my point is that we well, and then of
course you can talk about novels and plays

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:26.420
the same way, because there’s a lot more
to a novel than the words themselves.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:34.610
There’s you know, a novel is a deeply layered
thing, and part of your understanding of the

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:38.610
novel is your understanding of the interrelationships
between the layers, especially if it’s a

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:39.899
profound novel.

00:09:39.899 --> 00:09:45.890
I mean I think that’s why we have this sense
of depth in artistic and literary works.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:51.830
If the work is deep then it has many, many
layers, and the more layers it has interacting

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:56.970
at the same time, the more it’s a useful
representation of the essential elements of

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.740
being, and so the more meaningful it is to
us.

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:06.980
So, that’s all to say that there are modes
of communication that provide us with information

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:13.480
that we can’t articulate but that we still
act as if that information is valid.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:18.940
And you might say, well, valid for what, what
exactly is it doing, what is it representing,

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:23.170
what kind of information is it offering that’s
so compelling?

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:26.720
And what’s so interesting about it is the
kind of information that we’re talking,

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.760
that information is so compelling, that you’ll
pay to expose yourself to it.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:36.740
You know, it’s a rare lecture that you go
to and have to pay for the lecture itself.

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:40.540
I mean, you’ll come to the university and
you’ll get your degree, but if you had your

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:46.070
option on a Saturday afternoon, or a Saturday
night, let’s say, it’s relatively unlikely

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:49.290
that you would attend a lecture, and certainly
even more than unlikely that you would pay

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:54.100
to attend one, but you’ll certainly pay
to go somewhere to dance to music.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:57.750
And, you know, why that is, is not obvious.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.209
You say, well it’s entertaining.

00:10:59.209 --> 00:11:03.519
It’s like, yeah it’s entertaining alright
but it’s a lot more than that, and I think

00:11:03.519 --> 00:11:07.941
that dismissing things that are enjoyable
as entertainment, which means that they’re

00:11:07.941 --> 00:11:14.690
sort of peripheral, they’re unimportant
in some sense is a real, it’s a real terrible

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:16.310
habit of psychologists.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:20.250
So for example, when Steven Pinker wrote his
book…

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:25.630
it’s a book on language and unfortunately
I can’t remember its name at the moment…

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:30.410
he devoted one chapter at the end basically,
to non-verbal cultural forms and he talked

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.959
about them as epiphenomenal, fundamentally.

00:11:33.959 --> 00:11:38.080
You know, they’re just a by-product.

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:44.240
And I think that’s deeply wrong, I think
that our culture actually grew out of dance

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:54.470
and drama and music, and science came way,
way later than that.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.740
I was watching an old video today.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:03.019
It was of The Animals playing House of the
Rising Sun in 1964.

00:12:03.019 --> 00:12:05.360
It’s a pretty good video, and it’s a great
old song.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:09.720
I mean, they didn’t write it, it’s an
old blues song, but it’s a great old song

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.920
and they do a pretty good job of it.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.019
And the audience was full of girls and they
were screaming madly away.

00:12:16.019 --> 00:12:20.279
Now it wasn’t quite as bad as The Beatles,
which was completely unbelievable, but it

00:12:20.279 --> 00:12:25.920
was still, you know, a fairly continual din
of delighted shrieks.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:30.529
And, you know, I think that’s extremely
interesting because you’d never see that

00:12:30.529 --> 00:12:32.680
at a scientific conference [students’ laughter].

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:34.510
Well, it’s strange, eh?

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:40.610
Like, there isn’t anything else that elicits
that kind of response.

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:48.180
Maybe sports to some minimal degree, but certainly
with nowhere near the same amount of enthusiasm.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.700
And you know, I mean, that’s been happening
for a long time, it wasn’t merely a phenomenon

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:52.790
of the 60s.

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:57.089
I mean, I suspect it’s been happening ever
since there’s been wandering minstrels,

00:12:57.089 --> 00:12:58.950
and that’s a very, very long time.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:04.490
And I suspect that those wandering minstrels
left many offspring behind them [laughter].

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:09.079
And well, what’s interesting about that
is, obviously part of the reason that human

00:13:09.079 --> 00:13:12.290
beings can sing is because we’ve selected
each other for that ability, right?

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:17.339
I mean there’s something very attractive
about someone who’s artistically gifted.

00:13:17.339 --> 00:13:27.300
And the idea that women will do backflips
for a singer is not necessarily any negative

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:34.850
comment on their ability to evaluate what’s
important, it might be quite the reverse.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:38.149
So anyways, the point is that there’s lots
of different ways that we can communicate

00:13:38.149 --> 00:13:39.149
information.

00:13:39.149 --> 00:13:42.680
And you might say well it’s only simple
information that can be conveyed that way,

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:48.570
and you have to convey the complex information
in more formal forms, scientific and philosophical,

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.970
but I think that’s exactly backwards.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:56.620
I think we get to articulate the simple stuff
first, which is only what you expect, right?

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:02.120
Because when you’re mastering something
difficult, like the unfolding of being, the

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.980
probability that you’re going to get to
the simple things first and the complicated

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.870
things later is pretty much 100%.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:15.490
And I mean, different fields advance at different
speeds, but the artists and musicians get

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:21.260
to the complex matters far before the scientists
and the engineers.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:22.670
And it has to be that way.

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:30.050
One of the deep things I learned from Jung
was that, as our knowledge expands out into

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:35.160
the unknown, there are people operating at
the periphery, and so they really have one

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:38.550
foot in the unknown and one foot on dry land,
so to speak.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.080
And some of them are more in the water than
the others.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:45.600
And the ones almost submerged up to their
neck, when they tell you what they’re seeing,

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:51.089
it has to be poetic and musical and artistic,
because it’s so far beyond our capacity

00:14:51.089 --> 00:14:54.670
to articulate, that we can’t represent it
in words.

00:14:54.670 --> 00:15:00.180
And so all that can happen is that the artist
can get hold of it, and maybe the people who

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:06.139
are prone to religious-like sentiments, at
least experiences, which seems to be associated

00:15:06.139 --> 00:15:07.770
by the way with the trait openness.

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:12.500
To have the actual religious experience, rather
than to be an adherent of the dogma, which

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:14.870
is associated with low openness.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:21.660
So for Jung, it was the artists and the entrepreneurs,
because they’re the same people, who were

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:25.460
at the forefront of the expansion of what
we know into what is unknown.

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:30.620
And I think you can see echoes of that in
the way in which cities are rejuvenated.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:34.389
Because what happens, you can certainly see
this in Toronto, is that a part of the city

00:15:34.389 --> 00:15:40.600
that has some architectural or contextual
interest but is badly run down, starts to

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:41.600
get…

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:45.510
at some point, you know, the artistic people
show up and they think “Hey!

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:46.820
This is kind of cool.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:49.880
If we just did this to it there’d really
be something to it”.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:54.339
And it usually doesn’t hurt that the rents
are low because of course artists never have

00:15:54.339 --> 00:15:55.660
any money.

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:58.209
So then they go in there and civilise the
hell out of it.

00:15:58.209 --> 00:16:01.870
And of course, they’re renting, and that
jacks up the property value, and then the

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.990
people who are a little less open but kind
of interested in art start flowing in behind

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:06.990
them.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:11.610
And then soon the whole place is rejuvenated
and expensive, and of course the artists have

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:13.130
to go somewhere else.

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:17.290
But that’s a very interesting phenomenon,
because what’s happening is that even in

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:24.850
the cities they’re taking chaos and turning
it into order, and that’s what artists do.

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:31.710
Now that’s all to tell you that there are
different ways of transmitting and representing

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:38.579
information, and that you can incorporate
the information without any articulated notion

00:16:38.579 --> 00:16:40.339
of what you’re doing.

00:16:40.339 --> 00:16:44.459
So of course that’s the case, because otherwise
you’d have to claim that you understand

00:16:44.459 --> 00:16:51.269
everything that you do and can articulate
it, and that’s just wrong, you know.

00:16:51.269 --> 00:16:55.020
You have a theory of yourself but it’s not
a very accurate theory, which is why you’re

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:59.110
always doing things that are strange as far
as you’re concerned, which is also extremely

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:00.110
bizarre.

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:05.230
It’s like, who the hell are you if you can’t
even control your own behaviour?

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:08.480
And what does it mean to control it, and what
does it mean to not control it?

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:12.600
These are very, very complex and strange issues.

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:20.649
Alright, so I think the best way into this
is actually from an evolutionary perspective.

00:17:20.649 --> 00:17:27.510
And I’m not convinced that this is correct
but I think there’s something to it.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:38.240
So when you think about the nature of experience,
then you think, well the nature of experience

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:42.289
is quite different for different sorts of
creatures.

00:17:42.289 --> 00:17:45.779
And one of the primary differences between
creatures is the degree to which they’re

00:17:45.779 --> 00:17:50.330
capable of social interaction and then of
complex social interaction.

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:54.769
And primates generally speaking are very social
creatures.

00:17:54.769 --> 00:18:00.039
They live in troops, or packs, like dogs.

00:18:00.039 --> 00:18:03.109
Which is why we can get along with dogs, because
we understand each other.

00:18:03.109 --> 00:18:07.450
Being hierarchical troop animals, we understand
dogs, and they understand us, and they can

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:10.190
fit right in, pretty much.

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:16.490
And we’re very, very good at figuring out
where we are in the troop.

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:19.399
And by ‘we’ I mean primates.

00:18:19.399 --> 00:18:22.139
I mean that’s going back a very, very long
period of time.

00:18:22.139 --> 00:18:27.289
You know, monkeys and chimpanzees have very
sophisticated knowledge of the social structures

00:18:27.289 --> 00:18:28.480
surrounding them.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:32.090
They know who’s who, and they know what
status they are, they know what rank they

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.090
are, essentially.

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:37.090
And that’s also the case with people.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:42.929
And so one of the things you might say about
people is that our reality isn’t nature,

00:18:42.929 --> 00:18:43.929
exactly.

00:18:43.929 --> 00:18:45.779
It’s culture.

00:18:45.779 --> 00:18:50.960
Now, you can make the argument that you can’t
really distinguish nature from culture, and

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:56.279
I believe that that’s true and untrue, in
a sense.

00:18:56.279 --> 00:19:01.590
I think you can talk about the human and non-human
elements of reality.

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:06.529
And you can identify the human elements of
reality as culture, even though culture is

00:19:06.529 --> 00:19:11.989
so old that you can also think about it as
an inevitable part of nature.

00:19:11.989 --> 00:19:19.230
So anyway, so most of our experience in our
evolutionary past, at least let’s say for

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:24.989
the last several million years, we might as
well go back to when the ancestors of humans

00:19:24.989 --> 00:19:31.440
hypothetically split off from the ancestors
of chimpanzees.

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:36.210
And people know very accurately when these
sorts of splits occur, by the way, because

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:40.389
what they do is they have some sense of the
rate of transformation of DNA.

00:19:40.389 --> 00:19:44.800
You know it transforms and the rate is fairly
constant across time.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:50.630
And so if you take two creatures and you determine
how much genetic similarity there is between

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:56.220
them, and how much difference, you could calculate
how far back they diverged.

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:59.919
And so, the fossil record helps with that,
although it’s very sparse.

00:19:59.919 --> 00:20:04.889
But the DNA record is not very sparse at all,
in some sense, so you can be very accurate

00:20:04.889 --> 00:20:05.889
about it.

00:20:05.889 --> 00:20:11.349
We can even tell when people evolved or learned
to wear clothing, and the reason we can tell

00:20:11.349 --> 00:20:15.979
that is because there’s certain kinds of
lice that can only live on clothing, and they

00:20:15.979 --> 00:20:20.679
seem to have diverged from the lice that live
in hair about 50,000 years ago, something

00:20:20.679 --> 00:20:21.769
like that.

00:20:21.769 --> 00:20:25.200
And so the hypothesis is that, well, we must
have been figuring out how to wear clothes

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:27.869
at that point because the lice figured out
how to live in them.

00:20:27.869 --> 00:20:31.700
So, you know, it probably took them a little
while, but I doubt if it took them very long.

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:36.919
You know those little creatures that breed
fast, man those things can evolve quickly,

00:20:36.919 --> 00:20:38.899
much to our dismay.

00:20:38.899 --> 00:20:46.409
So okay, so a big part of the experiential
field of advanced primates is the social world.

00:20:46.409 --> 00:20:48.419
And so we could call that the primate world.

00:20:48.419 --> 00:20:53.139
And we could also say that another big part
of their experience is the non-primate world,

00:20:53.139 --> 00:20:56.340
which would be, roughly speaking, the natural
world.

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:02.289
And then we could also say that the other
elements of their experience that are very

00:21:02.289 --> 00:21:07.009
constant are their subjective being.

00:21:07.009 --> 00:21:13.239
And we don’t know much about the subjective
being of creatures like chimpanzees, although

00:21:13.239 --> 00:21:18.230
you can understand, I think, the subjective
being of a dog well enough to befriend the

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:19.259
dog.

00:21:19.259 --> 00:21:21.429
And so you have some insight.

00:21:21.429 --> 00:21:25.210
And I think it’s also reasonable to assume
that…

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:29.309
I mean for a long time people were unwilling
to admit that animals had a soul, that was

00:21:29.309 --> 00:21:30.309
the first problem.

00:21:30.309 --> 00:21:32.789
But after that, that they were conscious.

00:21:32.789 --> 00:21:34.779
And even the behaviourists fought that notion.

00:21:34.779 --> 00:21:40.009
But I think the simplest thing to do is to
assume that animals and human beings are the

00:21:40.009 --> 00:21:42.470
same, except when you can prove that they’re
different.

00:21:42.470 --> 00:21:46.849
Because we share so much of our evolutionary
past then the logical inference is that if

00:21:46.849 --> 00:21:51.169
you think a dog is doing something sort of
like you would do in that situation, and feeling

00:21:51.169 --> 00:21:55.580
that way, it probably is, with certain exceptions
for species difference.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:59.489
And if that wasn’t the case I don’t think
that you could have a relationship with the

00:21:59.489 --> 00:22:01.080
dog, or cat.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:06.590
You can’t have much of a relationship with
a lizard, although there are lizards, bearded

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:08.409
dragons in particular, that are social.

00:22:08.409 --> 00:22:11.249
They like to lay on top of each other and
they hang around together.

00:22:11.249 --> 00:22:13.909
And you can actually have more of a relationship
with them.

00:22:13.909 --> 00:22:16.440
And apparently the same is true for iguanas.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:24.830
So you can go quite a ways backwards in evolutionary
time, and still have enough similarity between

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:28.580
you and the creature that you’re interacting
with, so you can get a pretty good sense of

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:31.549
at least some element of their being.

00:22:31.549 --> 00:22:38.580
And so, the idea that subjective, or subjectivity,
is a very ancient part of our experience,

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:41.419
seems to me to be a perfectly reasonable supposition.

00:22:41.419 --> 00:22:46.190
We certainly know that it’s been around,
in all likelihood, for at least 150,000 years

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:51.220
(on the conservative side), because there
have been human beings that are essentially

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:56.700
identical to us, from a genetic perspective,
going back 150,000 years.

00:22:56.700 --> 00:23:01.820
But I think you’d have to be a pretty harsh
judge to assume that higher-order primates

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:04.899
like chimps don’t have some consciousness.

00:23:04.899 --> 00:23:09.960
And you know, and some limited self-consciousness.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:15.259
You know, if you mark a chimp’s nose with
lipstick and then show it a mirror, it will,

00:23:15.259 --> 00:23:18.200
at least sometimes, take the lipstick off
its nose.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:21.889
Although I’ve seen a gorilla try to fight
with a mirror.

00:23:21.889 --> 00:23:26.720
Well no, I haven’t personally seen that,
but I’ve seen a very good video of that

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:27.720
happening.

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:33.889
But I think the gorilla in it would eventually
figure out that it was him in the mirror.

00:23:33.889 --> 00:23:39.320
Dogs will recognise a dog in the mirror, but
they seem to ignore their reflection very,

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:40.320
very rapidly.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:44.059
And I don’t know if that means that they
have a very rudimentary self-consciousness,

00:23:44.059 --> 00:23:49.289
and that they figure out that that dog is
them, or if the dog just doesn’t smell like

00:23:49.289 --> 00:23:53.229
a dog, and then the dog thinks “well it
can’t be a dog because it doesn’t smell

00:23:53.229 --> 00:23:54.229
like a dog”.

00:23:54.229 --> 00:23:58.219
And dogs probably think that something is
a dog because of how it smells, and not because

00:23:58.219 --> 00:24:01.130
of how it looks.

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:05.399
I think that’s why dogs don’t seem to
have any sense of relative size.

00:24:05.399 --> 00:24:11.559
Because you can get a little Chihuahua barking
like mad at a Great Dane and you think, really?

00:24:11.559 --> 00:24:13.320
That’s going to work, is it?

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:17.539
But the dog doesn’t seem to have a clue
about that, like he’s perfectly happy.

00:24:17.539 --> 00:24:21.990
And the Great Dane, weirdly enough, will often
back off.

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:31.049
Anyway, okay, so we’ve been in a social
world, a deeply social world for a long time,

00:24:31.049 --> 00:24:35.009
and I’m also going to make the presupposition
that we’ve been in the stratified social

00:24:35.009 --> 00:24:36.779
world for a very long period of time.

00:24:36.779 --> 00:24:41.649
And I think you could call that, if you wanted
to, and I would never want to, by the way,

00:24:41.649 --> 00:24:45.109
you could call that the patriarchy.

00:24:45.109 --> 00:24:50.269
I think that I wouldn’t call it that because
that’s not what it is.

00:24:50.269 --> 00:24:58.349
But I think that the fact that it’s called
that, and that that’s accepted as a reasonable

00:24:58.349 --> 00:25:02.639
representation is actually a consequence of
the action of an archetype.

00:25:02.639 --> 00:25:08.219
And the archetype is that, it’s the archetype
of the terrible Great Father, actually.

00:25:08.219 --> 00:25:12.129
Because you think, well there’s a social
structure with striations in it, and that

00:25:12.129 --> 00:25:13.499
has advantages and disadvantages.

00:25:13.499 --> 00:25:16.349
And the advantages are that you can live in
it.

00:25:16.349 --> 00:25:20.059
And the disadvantages are that you have to
follow the damn rules, and they might not

00:25:20.059 --> 00:25:22.639
necessarily be to your individual benefit.

00:25:22.639 --> 00:25:30.659
So, within a social dominance hierarchy, there’s
always a bifurcated, it also always has bifurcated

00:25:30.659 --> 00:25:31.659
significance.

00:25:31.659 --> 00:25:38.360
It can be good, and is good, insofar as it
protects you from, say, from threat from without,

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:42.109
maybe from other primates invading your territory,
for example.

00:25:42.109 --> 00:25:49.190
But it can also be a very dismal structure
if you happen to be at the bottom of the hierarchy.

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:59.039
So anyways, as far as I can tell, that dominance
structure is typically represented as masculine.

00:25:59.039 --> 00:26:04.759
And I think the reason for that, although
I’m not absolutely certain of this, I think

00:26:04.759 --> 00:26:09.229
the reason for that is that our social hierarchies
are probably more like chimp social hierarchies

00:26:09.229 --> 00:26:11.389
than they are like bonobo social hierarchies.

00:26:11.389 --> 00:26:14.399
So, the bonobos are kind of a strange breed
of chimps.

00:26:14.399 --> 00:26:16.369
I don’t know how many of you know about
them.

00:26:16.369 --> 00:26:24.149
But the bonobos are extraordinarily sexual
and they use sex, pretty much as a standard

00:26:24.149 --> 00:26:27.009
means of communication and tension reduction.

00:26:27.009 --> 00:26:34.320
And there’s a lot of sex between the females
and the bonobo troops, and the use of sex

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:38.170
seems to bond them together in a way that
keeps male aggression under control, which

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:39.679
is quite interesting.

00:26:39.679 --> 00:26:45.880
But in the chimps the fundamental dominance
hierarchy is male, although there’s a female

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:51.000
dominance hierarchy as well, and some females
can certainly be more dominant than some males.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.710
But the fundamental structure seems to be
male.

00:26:53.710 --> 00:27:00.149
And then I would also say, well, it’s probably
dependent to some degree in the primate community,

00:27:00.149 --> 00:27:03.289
and in other social animals, on other factors.

00:27:03.289 --> 00:27:10.330
So we know, for example, that the gender that
has the highest level of testosterone tends

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:12.159
to be the dominant gender.

00:27:12.159 --> 00:27:16.259
And so in hyenas the females actually have
a higher level of testosterone.

00:27:16.259 --> 00:27:19.479
They’re actually bigger and more aggressive
than the males, and the price they pay for

00:27:19.479 --> 00:27:23.700
that is that they have to give birth through
a structure that’s very much like a penis,

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:29.539
which is not the world’s most pleasant experience,
and might account for the hyenas’ temper.

00:27:29.539 --> 00:27:34.240
But, in human beings, you know, you see sexual
dimorphism.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:40.249
Men are slightly bigger than women, and that’s
also generally characteristic of creatures

00:27:40.249 --> 00:27:44.519
that have a dominance hierarchy that’s tilted
towards masculine.

00:27:44.519 --> 00:27:52.519
And then the other factor seems to be that
human beings have… our infants have very,

00:27:52.519 --> 00:28:00.279
very, very, very long periods of dependence,
and it’s very difficult to manoeuver your

00:28:00.279 --> 00:28:06.969
way, doing anything, really, especially anything
that has to do with competition and power,

00:28:06.969 --> 00:28:12.789
if you’re taking care of one infant, let
alone three.

00:28:12.789 --> 00:28:22.279
And so, it seems to me that those are all
valid reasons why the primary power structures

00:28:22.279 --> 00:28:27.519
among human beings have typically been male,
and why they’re represented as masculine.

00:28:27.519 --> 00:28:31.519
Now there are other reasons too, because this
is a multi-faceted phenomenon.

00:28:31.519 --> 00:28:37.539
I mean, one of the things that’s interesting
about human females is that they’re selective

00:28:37.539 --> 00:28:38.989
maters.

00:28:38.989 --> 00:28:43.399
Now chimp females are not selective maters,
and what that means is that the dominant males

00:28:43.399 --> 00:28:46.960
still have most of the offspring, but the
reason for that, apparently, is because they

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:51.850
chase the subordinate males away from sexually
receptive females.

00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:55.970
Although the sexually receptive females will
sneak off behind the rocks somewhere and mate

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:58.809
with the subordinate male.

00:28:58.809 --> 00:29:03.549
But as long as the dominant male is around
the probability of that is quite low.

00:29:03.549 --> 00:29:08.759
So the female chimps aren’t sexually selective,
whereas human females have concealed ovulation,

00:29:08.759 --> 00:29:13.940
so that no one knows when they’re at their
most fertile, and they’re selective maters.

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:18.239
And women in every society virtually, that’s
ever been studies, have a typical pattern

00:29:18.239 --> 00:29:22.720
of behaviour, which is that if you look at
the female dominance hierarchy and you look

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:27.919
at the male dominance hierarchy, the females
will mate across and up dominance hierarchies,

00:29:27.919 --> 00:29:30.340
and the males will mate across and down.

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:35.289
Which works fine for both genders, because
their interests align.

00:29:35.289 --> 00:29:42.419
But it also means that in all likelihood women
have exerted tremendous sexual selection pressure

00:29:42.419 --> 00:29:47.519
on men, and that might be part of the reason,
of the many reasons, that we actually…

00:29:47.519 --> 00:29:51.700
you know the theory is that the common ancestor
between chimps and humans was probably a lot

00:29:51.700 --> 00:29:55.080
more like a chimp, a modern chimp, than like
a modern human.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:59.710
So for whatever reason, we’ve undergone
a lot more transformation in the last 7 million

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:01.360
years than chimps have.

00:30:01.360 --> 00:30:06.559
And one possible reason for that, and I think
it’s a highly probable reason, is that sexual

00:30:06.559 --> 00:30:11.100
selection operated a lot more viciously, so
to speak, among humans.

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:16.950
It’s operated to the point where you have
twice as many female ancestors as male.

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:20.759
And you might think, well that can’t be
possible, but here’s how it’s possible.

00:30:20.759 --> 00:30:26.519
It’s like, only every second man has children,
had a child, while all women had one.

00:30:26.519 --> 00:30:30.039
Now of course, that isn’t what happened,
but on average that’s what happened.

00:30:30.039 --> 00:30:36.570
So the proportion of men who are completely
childless in any given generation is far higher

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:39.039
than the proportion of women who are.

00:30:39.039 --> 00:30:45.460
So you see an element of expendability in
males, and that’s typical across the biological

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:48.840
community, because you don’t need as many
males as you need females in order to keep

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:52.080
the population moving forward.

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:55.950
And one of the things that happens as a consequence
of that, at least in principle, is that males

00:30:55.950 --> 00:31:01.580
are more behaviourally variable and variable
across a lot of dimensions, than females are.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:05.899
And that’s a plus, because they’re more
variable on the upside, but it’s also negative

00:31:05.899 --> 00:31:09.110
because they’re more prevalent on the downside.

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:13.739
Now there’s some substantial argument about
that in relationship to people, what the actual

00:31:13.739 --> 00:31:18.019
implications of that are for human beings.

00:31:18.019 --> 00:31:24.739
One potential implication is that although
women and men have equivalent IQs, the standard

00:31:24.739 --> 00:31:26.249
deviation is slightly different.

00:31:26.249 --> 00:31:30.659
Which means that there are more males who
are intellectually impaired, but there are

00:31:30.659 --> 00:31:35.149
also more males who are four and five standard
deviations above the population mean.

00:31:35.149 --> 00:31:40.700
You don’t need much of a difference in standard
deviation in order to produce radical differences

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:42.080
at the extreme.

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:45.539
So I’ll give you an example.

00:31:45.539 --> 00:31:50.940
Males are more aggressive than females among
humans, but the effect is only about half

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:52.149
a standard deviation.

00:31:52.149 --> 00:31:59.769
And so what that means is that, if you take
a female and a male randomly from a population

00:31:59.769 --> 00:32:04.840
and you were trying to guess who was the more
aggressive of the two, most of the time, you

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:10.190
know about 60% of the time, the male would
be more aggressive than the female, but 40%

00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:11.450
of the time it would be reversed.

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:13.850
So you know, that’s a lot of overlap.

00:32:13.850 --> 00:32:17.389
But then here’s the rub.

00:32:17.389 --> 00:32:22.629
So let’s say just for example, that among
the population of men and women combined,

00:32:22.629 --> 00:32:28.249
only the most aggressive person out of a hundred
ends up in jail.

00:32:28.249 --> 00:32:29.739
Those are all men.

00:32:29.739 --> 00:32:35.629
Because that tilt towards aggression is enough,
so that when you go way out on the distribution,

00:32:35.629 --> 00:32:39.820
the only people who are that extreme are men,
even though the population difference isn’t

00:32:39.820 --> 00:32:40.969
that great.

00:32:40.969 --> 00:32:47.349
So anyways, there’s a massive debate in
the relevant literature about the potential

00:32:47.349 --> 00:32:51.469
consequences of that for things like cognitive
function, because there’s some indication

00:32:51.469 --> 00:32:56.399
as well that men are more cognitively specialised
but women are more cognitively robust.

00:32:56.399 --> 00:33:00.239
Anyways, it doesn’t really matter.

00:33:00.239 --> 00:33:04.839
But what does matter is the fact of this permanent
dominance hierarchy.

00:33:04.839 --> 00:33:10.639
Now, the dominance hierarchy, the male dominance
hierarchy, also has another feature.

00:33:10.639 --> 00:33:15.849
So we already said that, you know, on average,
men are half as likely to leave offspring

00:33:15.849 --> 00:33:17.289
as women are.

00:33:17.289 --> 00:33:23.159
And so what that means is that all things
considered…

00:33:23.159 --> 00:33:24.159
this isn’t…

00:33:24.159 --> 00:33:28.179
I’m not trying to make this into a circular
argument.

00:33:28.179 --> 00:33:31.370
Because I could say it’s the more successful
males that leave more offspring.

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:34.959
But then you’d say, well that’s the definition,
Darwinian definition, of success.

00:33:34.959 --> 00:33:39.499
But I don’t mean Darwinian success, because
that’s self-evident.

00:33:39.499 --> 00:33:44.679
What I mean is, if you look in the modern
world for example, and you try to predict

00:33:44.679 --> 00:33:50.999
number of available sexual partners for a
given male, your best predictive measure of

00:33:50.999 --> 00:33:53.350
that is income.

00:33:53.350 --> 00:33:57.729
And the best predictive measure of that is
intelligence and conscientiousness.

00:33:57.729 --> 00:34:04.709
And so, what happens is that males orient
themselves in groups, and then they compete.

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:07.049
And then women peel off the top.

00:34:07.049 --> 00:34:13.929
And so the women are in a position of judgment
on men, and the judgment is the judgment of

00:34:13.929 --> 00:34:14.929
nature.

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:17.399
Now, from a Darwinian perspective…

00:34:17.399 --> 00:34:22.529
you know, when we think of nature we think
of, well it depends on who you are, but if

00:34:22.529 --> 00:34:26.800
you’re an environmentalist you think of
a French impressionist landscape, you know,

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:32.440
forgetting that nature is also malarial mosquitoes
and cancer, and rats infected with bubonic

00:34:32.440 --> 00:34:35.679
plague and all those other lovely things.

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:41.579
But, from a Darwinian perspective, you can
define nature much more straight-forwardly,

00:34:41.579 --> 00:34:44.830
and more accurately, as that which selects.

00:34:44.830 --> 00:34:50.460
Now, you know, you hear a lot about natural
selection, and natural selection basically

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:56.180
assumes that there is a random distribution
of alteration in genetic structure, in any

00:34:56.180 --> 00:35:00.420
population, in any generation.

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:05.440
And some of those random alterations will
be more suited to that particular environment,

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:09.740
suited being they’ll live and they’ll
reproduce.

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:16.950
And genetic transformation takes place across
the millennia as the organism chases the landscape,

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:17.950
roughly speaking.

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:25.670
But Darwin, you know, Darwin, who was an unbelievably
intelligent person, was also very, very interested

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:30.470
in sexual selection, and as far as Darwin
was concerned, sexual selection was just as

00:35:30.470 --> 00:35:38.819
powerful in modifying genetic structure, in
driving evolution, as natural selection.

00:35:38.819 --> 00:35:41.540
And that’s been very underplayed by biologists.

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:44.270
That’s only started to switch in the last…

00:35:44.270 --> 00:35:49.330
I would say it hasn’t switched that much
yet, but it started to switch probably in

00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:51.420
about the last 25 years.

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:58.910
And that’s because the idea of sexual selection
makes things a hell of a lot more complicated.

00:35:58.910 --> 00:36:04.460
Because, well first of all, in a standard
Darwinian account of evolution, there’s

00:36:04.460 --> 00:36:08.750
no place for mind, there’s no place for
a creator, there’s no place for teleology.

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:14.079
And teleology is something moving towards
an end, a goal, right?

00:36:14.079 --> 00:36:22.559
But the thing is, if sexual selection is operative,
and if consciousness is ancient, then mind

00:36:22.559 --> 00:36:27.550
has been operating through sexual selection
as long as there’s been sexual selection.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:34.220
So you think, do you have any choice, do you
make any choice in who your sexual partner

00:36:34.220 --> 00:36:35.220
will be?

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:41.380
And the answer to that is, well it’s hardly
random, you make a choice!

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:46.040
And what’s interesting is that, you know,
if you take 60 people, let’s say if you

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:49.220
took 60 women, and you showed them a bunch
of men, and you asked them to rank them in

00:36:49.220 --> 00:36:53.270
terms of their attractiveness, there’s going
to be very consistent rankings.

00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:58.490
You know, I mean, we know what makes up attractive
facial features, for women and for men, it’s

00:36:58.490 --> 00:36:59.529
easy to determine that.

00:36:59.529 --> 00:37:05.010
What you do is you take 60 faces and you average
the features.

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:09.390
So you don’t get the average person, because
the average person is more like a median,

00:37:09.390 --> 00:37:11.430
you get the averaged person.

00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:16.670
And the averaged person has perfectly symmetrical
features that are nicely shaped, and fairly

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:22.480
big eyes, and they’re very nice-looking,
they’re very attractive.

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:29.329
And so, that means that there’s like a central
human form, in a sense, that we find attractive.

00:37:29.329 --> 00:37:33.430
And we see this in other species, for example
there are butterflies who won’t mate with

00:37:33.430 --> 00:37:39.290
another butterfly if it’s like a sixteenth
of an inch out of symmetry, because it’s

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:41.660
not a butterfly of that type, you know.

00:37:41.660 --> 00:37:47.559
And that’s how people think too, if you
deviate from the averaged person, the less

00:37:47.559 --> 00:37:51.640
canonically human you appear, and the less
attractive you appear.

00:37:51.640 --> 00:37:55.750
And, you know, we’re chasing this ideal
in some sense that’s an emergent property

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:57.940
of the nature of our species.

00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:04.620
And then, you know, there’s certain physical
characteristics, wide shoulders in men, and

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:05.620
narrow waists.

00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:10.019
In women, it’s waist-to-hip ratio, a very
common marker of beauty, across cultures,

00:38:10.019 --> 00:38:13.109
and across body types, interestingly enough.

00:38:13.109 --> 00:38:18.890
So, if you take thin women and heavier women,
and you get men to rate the attractiveness

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:23.430
of the women within that category, the heavy
women and the light women who have a waist-to-hip

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:27.750
ratio of about 0.68 are the ones that are
judged most attractive physically.

00:38:27.750 --> 00:38:33.059
And so, that actually correlates, by the way,
with fertility, because the abdominal fat

00:38:33.059 --> 00:38:38.790
in a young woman is a sign of ill health and
also a marker of decreased likelihood of conception.

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:43.730
Now none of this is operating consciously,
obviously, it’s deeply wired into us, it’s

00:38:43.730 --> 00:38:46.779
part of our mediate perception.

00:38:46.779 --> 00:38:52.220
But it still does indicate that there’s
an ideal, like a platonic ideal, lurking at

00:38:52.220 --> 00:38:56.510
the back of our minds, against whom we compare
everyone that we meet.

00:38:56.510 --> 00:39:00.750
And then you might say, well what’s the
nature of that platonic ideal, and that’s

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:03.420
a very, very complicated question.

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:12.670
You know, I would say that you could almost
literally claim that the…

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:22.410
well you can certainly claim that the ideal
male is represented in mythology as a hero.

00:39:22.410 --> 00:39:25.950
And that’s actually what mythology is about,
it’s about representing ideal patterns of

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:28.450
behaviour, so it’s hardly surprising.

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:33.390
And so, you know, if you go to a movie, and
it’s a romance, and there’s the main lead

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:37.700
character that you’re supposed to fall in
love with (if you happen to be the kind of

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:44.000
person that would fall in love with that kind
of person), then he’s going to act out a

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:45.700
particular pattern of behaviour.

00:39:45.700 --> 00:39:48.280
And the pattern of behaviour is quite identifiable.

00:39:48.280 --> 00:39:53.390
So for example he’s going to move forward
and explore, and not hide and cower.

00:39:53.390 --> 00:39:56.650
And the probability that he’s going to be
creative is very high, and the probability

00:39:56.650 --> 00:40:00.000
that he’s going to be good-looking and strong
is very high.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.420
And so, those are archetypal features.

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:09.450
And those aren’t all the archetypal features,
because those are, in some sense, those are

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:11.530
the self-evident ones.

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:16.539
But you know, people are also evaluating each
other for things like intelligence, and personality,

00:40:16.539 --> 00:40:18.319
and character.

00:40:18.319 --> 00:40:24.880
And you could say in some sense that men are
competing to be the best man, and women are

00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:29.789
watching the competition to take the man who
wins on the presupposition that he wouldn’t

00:40:29.789 --> 00:40:31.029
win if he wasn’t the best man.

00:40:31.029 --> 00:40:34.200
It’s a very, very intelligent strategy.

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:36.700
Because why not outsource the problem.

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:42.309
Let the men sort it out, well as I say, it’s
too cognitively complex to compute.

00:40:42.309 --> 00:40:45.859
You could say that the male dominance hierarchy
is equivalent to the stock market.

00:40:45.859 --> 00:40:48.609
It’s exactly equivalent to the stock market.

00:40:48.609 --> 00:40:54.809
The stocks are always competing with one another,
and with every other commodity, for primacy

00:40:54.809 --> 00:40:59.660
of price and value, and that’s exactly what
happens with male competition.

00:40:59.660 --> 00:41:04.130
So one of the things that we’ve just discovered
in my lab, this is Caitlyn Burton’s work,

00:41:04.130 --> 00:41:09.420
it’s very cool, is that we took… we were
trying to understand the fundamental sub-structure

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:13.400
of conservatism vs. liberalism.

00:41:13.400 --> 00:41:18.440
And I’m going to speak in terms of conservatism,
because that’s how we construed the data,

00:41:18.440 --> 00:41:21.039
although we could’ve done the reverse if
we were going to construe liberalism.

00:41:21.039 --> 00:41:25.781
And what we did was we took, we got a bunch
of people to sit down and write down a bunch

00:41:25.781 --> 00:41:30.230
of statements that they though conservatives
vs. liberals would disagree with.

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:33.769
You know, I think we had, I don’t remember
how many statements, 300 statements, something

00:41:33.769 --> 00:41:34.769
like that.

00:41:34.769 --> 00:41:38.160
We had a lot of people generate them and take
them from news items and so forth, because

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:42.930
we didn’t want any bias in the initial question
set.

00:41:42.930 --> 00:41:48.290
Or we didn’t want a bias that wasn’t there
in the actual world.

00:41:48.290 --> 00:41:53.109
So we had many people do this, and then we
gave these questions to many, many people

00:41:53.109 --> 00:41:56.549
online, in a variety of stages.

00:41:56.549 --> 00:42:03.450
And we extracted those out that seemed to
best fit the data, and then assessed them

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:07.690
for the utility in predicting things like
party membership or voting behaviour.

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:11.150
And so we got a good structure, it makes a
lot of sense, and what we found with regards

00:42:11.150 --> 00:42:18.480
to conservatism was that there was an ethnocentrism
factor, it was the third and weakest factor,

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:23.130
and that would be associated with in-group
preference vs. out-group derogation.

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:29.700
So those are anti-immigrant people, fundamentally,
you know, and they’re, well they’re ethnocentric.

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:36.420
That’s the simplest way to explain it.

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:42.640
And then the second smallest factor was religious
traditionalism.

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:46.640
And most of this was done with Americans,
for a variety of reasons.

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:49.609
And that was really where the fundamentalist
Christians nested.

00:42:49.609 --> 00:42:57.390
And not everyone… that was not only a factor
made up of fundamentalist Christian beliefs,

00:42:57.390 --> 00:42:59.700
but they loaded heavily.

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:04.009
Those were people who weren’t for stem cell
research, they were anti-abortion, they were

00:43:04.009 --> 00:43:06.109
anti-gay marriage.

00:43:06.109 --> 00:43:10.569
Or they were pro-traditional, which is how
they see themselves.

00:43:10.569 --> 00:43:19.019
And then, the largest factor was a factor
that we called ‘masculine independence’.

00:43:19.019 --> 00:43:25.089
And it was more characteristic of men, and
what it basically consisted of was an attitude

00:43:25.089 --> 00:43:28.790
that winners win and losers lose, and that’s
just how it should be.

00:43:28.790 --> 00:43:36.529
So it was the men who… the personality predictors,
one of them was extroverted assertiveness…

00:43:36.529 --> 00:43:44.339
hunting and the right to own firearms also
loaded on this factor, but it was masculine

00:43:44.339 --> 00:43:48.770
competitiveness, I think, is the best way
of thinking about it.

00:43:48.770 --> 00:43:56.010
And I think it’s a good illustration of
the kind of individual attitude that makes

00:43:56.010 --> 00:44:00.849
you more likely to attempt to climb up dominance
hierarchies and dominate them from the top

00:44:00.849 --> 00:44:01.849
down.

00:44:01.849 --> 00:44:05.400
And that’s not necessarily something that
would be associated with factors like compassion.

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:09.750
Well, I’ll leave it at that.

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:11.619
It’s just that you can see…

00:44:11.619 --> 00:44:16.190
I’m telling you that because you can see
how these proclivities manifest themselves

00:44:16.190 --> 00:44:18.720
in all sorts of areas of behaviour.

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:25.300
I can tell you something else that’s very
interesting about men and violence.

00:44:25.300 --> 00:44:39.930
I don’t know if I can get this story straight
because it’s been a while since I told it.

00:44:39.930 --> 00:44:46.839
So there’s this interesting phenomenon,
that’s very characteristic of societies,

00:44:46.839 --> 00:44:50.990
I believe pretty much everywhere it’s been
studied.

00:44:50.990 --> 00:44:54.569
Now, you can calculate an index called the
Gini coefficient.

00:44:54.569 --> 00:45:00.779
And the Gini coefficient is a number that
represents how much inequality of income distribution

00:45:00.779 --> 00:45:03.089
there is in a given geographical area.

00:45:03.089 --> 00:45:09.780
So you could calculate a Gini coefficient
for a street or an area in a city, or a city,

00:45:09.780 --> 00:45:13.050
or a state, or a country, you can do it at
all those levels.

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:18.690
And what you find is that, you know you always
hear this idea that poverty causes crime.

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:20.800
That’s a classic left-wing idea.

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:24.210
But it’s wrong, it’s seriously wrong.

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:25.780
And it’s importantly wrong.

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:29.450
And it’s definitively wrong, not only that.

00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:33.009
So there’s no argument about this, it’s
already been established.

00:45:33.009 --> 00:45:38.009
What causes crime, especially aggressive crime,
is relative poverty, and relative poverty

00:45:38.009 --> 00:45:40.099
is not the same thing as poverty at all.

00:45:40.099 --> 00:45:42.359
It’s seriously not the same thing.

00:45:42.359 --> 00:45:44.410
Poverty is when you don’t have enough to
eat.

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:49.519
Relative poverty is when the guy next door
has a much better car than you.

00:45:49.519 --> 00:45:52.980
And there’s lots of relative poverty in
the United States, and there’s some absolute

00:45:52.980 --> 00:45:57.180
poverty, but even the absolute poverty in
the United States is nothing like the absolute

00:45:57.180 --> 00:46:02.480
poverty say in places like India or sub-Saharan
Africa, where absolute poverty means you have

00:46:02.480 --> 00:46:03.480
nothing.

00:46:03.480 --> 00:46:09.430
Now, what’s really interesting about the
Gini coefficient is that if you go to places

00:46:09.430 --> 00:46:15.650
where everyone, roughly speaking, is poor,
say by national standards…

00:46:15.650 --> 00:46:21.340
I think South Dakota was often used as an
example, or maybe one of the Maritime Provinces…

00:46:21.340 --> 00:46:26.430
like Newfoundland, where there’s low average
income but it’s pretty flat distribution,

00:46:26.430 --> 00:46:29.030
there’s almost no crime.

00:46:29.030 --> 00:46:34.640
And if you go to places where everyone’s
rich then there’s almost no crime.

00:46:34.640 --> 00:46:40.200
But if you go places where there’s poor
people and moderately well-off people, and

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:45.880
rich people, and the distribution is really
steep, then the rate of aggressive behaviour

00:46:45.880 --> 00:46:50.470
among young men, and it’s usually within
their own ethnic group, starts to sky-rocket

00:46:50.470 --> 00:46:52.420
out of control.

00:46:52.420 --> 00:46:57.200
And the reason for that seems to be that,
if the dominance hierarchy is too steep, then

00:46:57.200 --> 00:47:03.390
the young men have no likelihood of climbing
to a dominant position while playing the standard

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:04.789
social game.

00:47:04.789 --> 00:47:09.759
And so what they do is turn to aggression,
to make their mark on the world.

00:47:09.759 --> 00:47:12.039
And it works, too, that’s the other thing.

00:47:12.039 --> 00:47:17.250
Make no mistake about it, if you’re looking
for status where status is hard to achieve,

00:47:17.250 --> 00:47:22.750
and you’re the meanest, toughest guy around,
and, you know, around a bunch of people who,

00:47:22.750 --> 00:47:26.819
like you, don’t have much money, then you’re
going to benefit from that status.

00:47:26.819 --> 00:47:27.819
It works.

00:47:27.819 --> 00:47:28.819
Yeah?

00:47:28.819 --> 00:47:33.510
Would that be part of the reason why… because
I know that, I think that violent crime rate

00:47:33.510 --> 00:47:38.940
in the US is higher than it is in Canada,
would that be part of the reason why?

00:47:38.940 --> 00:47:44.420
Sure, yeah, I mean you can make a real conservative
argument for making sure that, you know that

00:47:44.420 --> 00:47:48.450
conservatives are very anti-income distribution,
and we figure that’s because of the guys

00:47:48.450 --> 00:47:53.339
that have this male independence, you know
they have this male independence factor, they

00:47:53.339 --> 00:47:57.070
don’t want to be distributing resources
to people who are down the dominance hierarchy

00:47:57.070 --> 00:47:59.690
because they want them down the dominance
hierarchy!

00:47:59.690 --> 00:48:03.849
They want there to be a difference between
the people on top and the people on the bottom,

00:48:03.849 --> 00:48:08.060
so that they can be the people on top, so
that it increases their relative attractiveness.

00:48:08.060 --> 00:48:11.170
Like it’s a perfectly logical game.

00:48:11.170 --> 00:48:15.680
And they presume that, well the rules are
set up, and like every man can go for it,

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:19.980
do his best, and the winner wins and the loser
loses, and that’s just how it is, and “don’t

00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:22.380
ask me to fix it because I don’t want to.

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:28.569
And besides that I find it distasteful to
attempt to fix it”, more than that, right,

00:48:28.569 --> 00:48:34.240
because it’s a moral issue, it’s not just
an intellectual issue.

00:48:34.240 --> 00:48:39.549
So you can make a case, however, from the
conservative point of view, especially with

00:48:39.549 --> 00:48:45.599
regards to, say, beliefs in religious traditionalism,
and the desire to maintain social stability,

00:48:45.599 --> 00:48:49.480
that you shouldn’t let income distribution
become too unequal.

00:48:49.480 --> 00:48:53.759
Like one of the big things your society has
to do is to make sure that doesn’t get out

00:48:53.759 --> 00:48:58.259
of hand, because it tends to get out of hand,
it tends towards a few people having everything

00:48:58.259 --> 00:49:01.619
and almost everyone else having nothing.

00:49:01.619 --> 00:49:06.539
In a sense, it’s a natural consequence of
economic progression, which is actually something

00:49:06.539 --> 00:49:07.930
that Marx pointed out.

00:49:07.930 --> 00:49:11.900
Although an Italian named Pareto had figured
it out at approximately the same time, and

00:49:11.900 --> 00:49:15.440
I think with a lot more conceptual clarity.

00:49:15.440 --> 00:49:21.359
But, the more unequal you let your society
get, the higher the probability of death,

00:49:21.359 --> 00:49:24.009
roughly speaking, through violent causes.

00:49:24.009 --> 00:49:32.030
But I’m telling you why it is that, you
know, men want to climb the dominance hierarchy,

00:49:32.030 --> 00:49:33.940
and the reason they want to climb the dominance
hierarchy is that they get access to women.

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:37.039
I was going to, actually ask earlier in the
discussion, when you were talking about the

00:49:37.039 --> 00:49:47.039
familiar and the unfamiliar and the structure.

00:49:47.039 --> 00:49:52.924
So it seems like we all want to live within
a structure, but we don’t want the rules

00:49:52.924 --> 00:49:53.924
to apply to ourselves.

00:49:53.924 --> 00:49:56.440
Well, we have this contradictory problem,
we want to be protected by the structure but

00:49:56.440 --> 00:49:59.009
we want to advance our position within it.

00:49:59.009 --> 00:50:04.640
And so that means, what that should mean,
and this is I think the definition of civilised

00:50:04.640 --> 00:50:09.069
behaviour, is that you’re allowed to advance
your position within the structure as long

00:50:09.069 --> 00:50:12.530
as you don’t disrupt it negatively.

00:50:12.530 --> 00:50:14.130
And I think that most people do do that.

00:50:14.130 --> 00:50:21.079
In fact I think people in civilised countries
do that so effectively that it’s an absolute,

00:50:21.079 --> 00:50:23.240
incomprehensible miracle.

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:26.930
I can’t understand how or why it ever got
established.

00:50:26.930 --> 00:50:31.340
But like a psychopath will climb the ladder
and cut the rungs off underneath him, fundamentally.

00:50:31.340 --> 00:50:35.710
He doesn’t even care if the damn thing maintains
itself.

00:50:35.710 --> 00:50:40.609
You know, he’s perfectly willing to have
it destroyed after he’s exhausted it.

00:50:40.609 --> 00:50:45.440
But if everyone acted like that or even if
a substantial number of people acted like

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:51.160
that, the whole thing would come to a halt,
virtually in no time flat.

00:50:51.160 --> 00:50:59.039
So I mean, why… see, you might…

00:50:59.039 --> 00:51:01.079
here’s a reason…

00:51:01.079 --> 00:51:04.980
you know, because one of the things we were
talking about is masculine violence.

00:51:04.980 --> 00:51:08.789
Now the thing about masculine violence is
that it only tends to emerge in situations

00:51:08.789 --> 00:51:16.980
where there don’t seem to be any other reasonably
viable means of advancing status.

00:51:16.980 --> 00:51:20.299
So it’s not reasonable to say that men are
aggressive.

00:51:20.299 --> 00:51:23.940
You can say that on average men are more aggressive
than women.

00:51:23.940 --> 00:51:30.040
And you can also say that if you put men in
a situation where they have no, where they

00:51:30.040 --> 00:51:36.391
can see status differences but they have no
means of moving forward, that they’re likely

00:51:36.391 --> 00:51:40.960
to turn to aggression as a way of establishing
dominance.

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:45.789
And then you can say that, the reason for
that is that it makes them more attractive,

00:51:45.789 --> 00:51:47.609
the fundamental reason.

00:51:47.609 --> 00:51:48.869
Yeah?

00:51:48.869 --> 00:52:01.573
Yeah, I’d just like to add on, I read this
article once that talks about how polygamous

00:52:01.573 --> 00:52:02.573
societies are more violent than monogamous
ones.

00:52:02.573 --> 00:52:03.573
Yeah, absolutely.

00:52:03.573 --> 00:52:04.573
I was going to say maybe it’s because when
one guy has two wives that means that 50%

00:52:04.573 --> 00:52:05.573
of the population has no wives, no access
to sex.

00:52:05.573 --> 00:52:08.550
Yeah that’s exactly right, the evolutionary
psychology explanation for the pathology of

00:52:08.550 --> 00:52:12.880
polygamy is that once you let it establish
itself then then men get ultra-violent.

00:52:12.880 --> 00:52:18.130
So would you say that monogamy would almost
be a basis for civilised society?

00:52:18.130 --> 00:52:22.260
Many people have said that, and yes I think
you can make a strong case for that.

00:52:22.260 --> 00:52:25.660
And I think the fundamental reason is the
one you just pointed out.

00:52:25.660 --> 00:52:34.079
You know the idea is, well, would you rather
have one woman, or die, you know?

00:52:34.079 --> 00:52:38.780
Sorry no, that’s not quite right.

00:52:38.780 --> 00:52:44.259
It’s more like, would you be willing to
limit yourself to one partner or have a shot

00:52:44.259 --> 00:52:47.950
at many partners but a much higher probability
of dying.

00:52:47.950 --> 00:52:50.999
Right, and you know, some guys will take that.

00:52:50.999 --> 00:52:54.570
They’ll take the high-risk approach.

00:52:54.570 --> 00:53:00.450
So now, this doesn’t eliminate the difference
in individual differences in determining who’s

00:53:00.450 --> 00:53:05.240
going to be aggressive, because what will
happen is that as the Gini coefficient pressure

00:53:05.240 --> 00:53:10.410
rises, the more aggressive men, the men who
are more aggressive by nature, will get more

00:53:10.410 --> 00:53:11.670
aggressive first.

00:53:11.670 --> 00:53:15.059
So you can imagine, it’s a threshold phenomenon,
in some sense.

00:53:15.059 --> 00:53:19.720
And what I should tell you as well is that
the relationship between the Gini coefficient

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:26.490
and male-on-male homicide isn’t 0.2 or 0.3,
which is the correlation that you’d get

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:29.440
if you were predicting something like that
using personality.

00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:31.599
It’s like 0.8 or 0.9.

00:53:31.599 --> 00:53:35.740
It’s like it eats up all of it, it’s the
explanation.

00:53:35.740 --> 00:53:40.569
So it’s a huge effect, it’s so big an
effect that you can basically say that oh

00:53:40.569 --> 00:53:41.789
well, we figured that out.

00:53:41.789 --> 00:53:45.999
Although psychologists never know when they’ve
figured anything out, and they keep endlessly

00:53:45.999 --> 00:53:49.640
retesting it over and over and over, because
you know, we don’t know how to bring our

00:53:49.640 --> 00:53:50.900
science to a stop.

00:53:50.900 --> 00:53:55.109
But if you don’t accept the Gini coefficient-aggression
data, that’s like, you might as well throw

00:53:55.109 --> 00:54:01.109
the rest of social sciences out the window,
because the effect is unbelievably powerful.

00:54:01.109 --> 00:54:03.950
What geographical area does this take place
in?

00:54:03.950 --> 00:54:04.950
It depends…

00:54:04.950 --> 00:54:07.970
you can do it on any level of analysis.

00:54:07.970 --> 00:54:12.430
You can do it by county, you can do it by
city, you can do it by state, and you can

00:54:12.430 --> 00:54:14.940
do it by country, and it works on all of those
levels

00:54:14.940 --> 00:54:18.160
Student: Like, it predicts aggression, almost?

00:54:18.160 --> 00:54:19.240
You bet.

00:54:19.240 --> 00:54:20.240
Yeah.

00:54:20.240 --> 00:54:21.240
And that’s a great question.

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:27.670
I mean, the methodologically sophisticated
studies have done exactly that, to ensure

00:54:27.670 --> 00:54:31.309
that it’s actually this phenomenon rather
than other factors that might be operative

00:54:31.309 --> 00:54:33.880
in that particular geographical area.

00:54:33.880 --> 00:54:38.869
So countries with a higher Gini coefficient
are more violent, and cities within that country

00:54:38.869 --> 00:54:42.809
that have a higher than average Gini coefficient
for that country are more violent on average.

00:54:42.809 --> 00:54:46.250
It’s a very, very robust finding.

00:54:46.250 --> 00:54:56.099
Alright, so we’re going to say, for the
sake of argument, that you’ve got the male

00:54:56.099 --> 00:54:59.869
dominance hierarchy, and it’s represented
as masculine.

00:54:59.869 --> 00:55:05.500
Now, one of the things Jung said, now he thought
that women carried an image of men in their

00:55:05.500 --> 00:55:06.549
unconscious.

00:55:06.549 --> 00:55:12.549
And he thought that the image that women carried
of men in their unconscious was a group of

00:55:12.549 --> 00:55:14.460
men, not an individual man.

00:55:14.460 --> 00:55:17.220
He called that the animus.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:23.220
Whereas he believed that the image of women
that men carried in their collective unconscious

00:55:23.220 --> 00:55:26.690
was of a single woman, and he called that
the anima.

00:55:26.690 --> 00:55:31.690
Now I’m just telling you that, I’m not
going to justify it, or even discuss it, but

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:36.190
I just want you to keep that in the back of
your mind.

00:55:36.190 --> 00:55:41.950
So I guess part of the reason I’m telling
you that, come to think of it, is that I think

00:55:41.950 --> 00:55:48.359
it may provide some insight into why the idea
of the patriarchy has become such a well-accepted

00:55:48.359 --> 00:55:50.809
notion.

00:55:50.809 --> 00:55:53.960
Because it is an archetypal notion, but it’s
a one-sided archetypal notion.

00:55:53.960 --> 00:55:58.170
Okay, so back to sexual selection.

00:55:58.170 --> 00:56:04.819
Now, we already talked about the fact that
biologists perhaps were uncomfortable with

00:56:04.819 --> 00:56:10.130
the idea of sexual selection, because it brings
a non-random factor into evolution.

00:56:10.130 --> 00:56:17.499
A seriously non-random factor, because you
have to go back as far as you can in history

00:56:17.499 --> 00:56:23.019
to where there was no choice, on the part
of the organism, to attribute evolution to

00:56:23.019 --> 00:56:24.989
natural selection alone.

00:56:24.989 --> 00:56:28.680
And you have to go back a long ways back,
as I said, even butterflies are perfectly

00:56:28.680 --> 00:56:33.049
capable of distinguishing between a high-quality
butterfly partner, in butterfly terms, and

00:56:33.049 --> 00:56:34.980
a low-quality butterfly partner.

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:40.130
And you know, insects have been around for
a very, very long time.

00:56:40.130 --> 00:56:45.819
So we have no idea, what that actually means
is that we have no idea what role choice has

00:56:45.819 --> 00:56:48.819
made in the evolutionary process.

00:56:48.819 --> 00:56:52.799
But one thing that you can infer is that the
reason human beings are the way they are now,

00:56:52.799 --> 00:57:00.760
and not like chimps or human ancestors, is
because the sexual selection process got started,

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:02.819
and it was intense.

00:57:02.819 --> 00:57:05.670
And so, as a consequence of that, here we
are.

00:57:05.670 --> 00:57:07.579
And so, you know, maybe it was a good thing.

00:57:07.579 --> 00:57:10.970
Although it’s very hard, in some sense,
on all those people who failed.

00:57:10.970 --> 00:57:17.730
And that’s, you know, roughly speaking,
twice as many men as women.

00:57:17.730 --> 00:57:23.220
So then that all raises the question, like
who exactly is responsible for the male dominance

00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:24.220
hierarchy?

00:57:24.220 --> 00:57:27.480
Because you could say it’s male competitiveness,
but you could also say, yeah well it’s an

00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:30.810
inevitable function of female selection.

00:57:30.810 --> 00:57:33.750
Which is not an argument that you hear very
often.

00:57:33.750 --> 00:57:36.119
But I think it’s a very difficult argument
to escape from.

00:57:36.119 --> 00:57:37.730
And that leads us to our next hypothesis.

00:57:37.730 --> 00:57:43.240
So we’re going to say that the masculine
dominance hierarchy is represented as masculine.

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:45.789
I’m going to call that The Great Father
for the time being.

00:57:45.789 --> 00:57:51.150
And that’s the permanent dominance hierarchy
of men, and it’s always there, it moves

00:57:51.150 --> 00:57:52.150
through history.

00:57:52.150 --> 00:57:56.880
It’s different men all the time, but the
men slot in and out as they are born and die,

00:57:56.880 --> 00:58:00.369
but the structure itself stays intact across…

00:58:00.369 --> 00:58:01.369
forever.

00:58:01.369 --> 00:58:02.579
It’s always there.

00:58:02.579 --> 00:58:06.810
It’s been there at least for millions of
years, so for our purposes we’ll just call

00:58:06.810 --> 00:58:07.810
it permanent.

00:58:07.810 --> 00:58:11.680
It’s a permanent part of experience, and
it’s a big part of it.

00:58:11.680 --> 00:58:16.490
And you know, as culture gets more and more…

00:58:16.490 --> 00:58:20.509
covers larger and larger expanses of territory,
gets more and more sophisticated, it’s an

00:58:20.509 --> 00:58:22.890
ever larger part of reality.

00:58:22.890 --> 00:58:30.819
I mean, most of us spend almost all our time
coping with the dominance hierarchy, and almost

00:58:30.819 --> 00:58:32.890
none of our time combatting nature.

00:58:32.890 --> 00:58:38.309
You know, you get wet for a little while and
that’s it, you know, but most of the problems

00:58:38.309 --> 00:58:44.029
that you would face in a purely natural environment,
you’re so distant from them that you could

00:58:44.029 --> 00:58:47.880
hardly imagine, you can’t even imagine what
it would be like, in some sense, to be in

00:58:47.880 --> 00:58:48.880
an environment like that.

00:58:48.880 --> 00:58:49.880
Yeah?

00:58:49.880 --> 00:58:50.880
I have a question.

00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:51.880
Like your explanation of what drives men to
become competitive and vindictive of the hierarchy…

00:58:51.880 --> 00:58:52.880
what drives women?

00:58:52.880 --> 00:58:53.880
Like for example there’s an aggressive nature
of women and there’s some sort of competitiveness

00:58:53.880 --> 00:58:54.880
in women, let’s say at the University of
Toronto.

00:58:54.880 --> 00:59:05.190
So 

00:59:05.190 --> 00:59:09.390
what is driving women to try to elevate their
level in the hierarchy?

00:59:09.390 --> 00:59:10.390
Well, that’s a good question.

00:59:10.390 --> 00:59:13.720
So the way I would answer that question is
two-fold.

00:59:13.720 --> 00:59:19.519
So the question was what drives women to move
to the top of the hierarchy?

00:59:19.519 --> 00:59:24.089
It’s something that’s worth discussing.

00:59:24.089 --> 00:59:27.950
So if anybody objects to what I’m going
to say, then please do, because I’m not,

00:59:27.950 --> 00:59:31.099
you know, dishing this out as received truth,
it’s…

00:59:31.099 --> 00:59:34.410
I’ve been trying to figure this out and
this is what it looks like to me.

00:59:34.410 --> 00:59:37.980
The first thing is that I think there are…
male and female dominance hierarchies both

00:59:37.980 --> 00:59:40.279
exist but they’re different.

00:59:40.279 --> 00:59:45.520
And that females compete with each other intensely,
but they don’t compete for the same things,

00:59:45.520 --> 00:59:48.200
and they don’t compete the same way.

00:59:48.200 --> 00:59:54.780
So let me tell you a little story, this is
a bit of a divergence, but it’s an interesting

00:59:54.780 --> 00:59:55.780
point.

00:59:55.780 --> 00:59:58.890
And one of the questions I’ve always been
asked in this class… because I’m going

00:59:58.890 --> 01:00:04.470
to lay out a hero story, and the fundamental
hero archetype, and the hero is masculine

01:00:04.470 --> 01:00:08.400
in mythology… and so the women always ask,
well what about the role of the woman?

01:00:08.400 --> 01:00:12.089
And it’s like, it’s very, very complex,
which of course, all you women already know,

01:00:12.089 --> 01:00:13.839
because it is very, very complex.

01:00:13.839 --> 01:00:16.529
But it isn’t something that…

01:00:16.529 --> 01:00:20.160
first of all I don’t think it’s a question
that would’ve been asked before the invention

01:00:20.160 --> 01:00:22.790
of the birth control pill.

01:00:22.790 --> 01:00:27.260
Because we know what the archetypal female
is, prior to that.

01:00:27.260 --> 01:00:30.710
It’s the Virgin Mary with child, it’s
a virgin with child.

01:00:30.710 --> 01:00:35.579
Which means that the unit for woman is ‘woman
with child’.

01:00:35.579 --> 01:00:38.099
It’s not woman, it’s woman with child.

01:00:38.099 --> 01:00:44.859
And, well, it’s obvious why that is, because
as soon as you become a woman, in most societies,

01:00:44.859 --> 01:00:46.260
you have a child.

01:00:46.260 --> 01:00:52.609
Before that, you’re a girl, and that’s,
you know, that’s in some sense irrelevant,

01:00:52.609 --> 01:00:54.390
in terms of your destiny.

01:00:54.390 --> 01:01:00.590
Now you might say, well what’s your archetypal
pattern if you’re not a mother?

01:01:00.590 --> 01:01:08.180
Well, the way it looks to me is that there’s
two archetypes for personal development, for

01:01:08.180 --> 01:01:10.170
the personal path, roughly speaking.

01:01:10.170 --> 01:01:15.970
There’s the hero, and that would be the
person that explores the unknown and discovers

01:01:15.970 --> 01:01:19.430
something of value and brings it back and
distributes it to the community.

01:01:19.430 --> 01:01:20.970
So it’s like a hunting…

01:01:20.970 --> 01:01:29.739
it’s probably predicated on a hunting platform,
because our bodies are hunting platforms,

01:01:29.739 --> 01:01:31.450
basically.

01:01:31.450 --> 01:01:36.170
And one theory about what men did, which is
a very probable theory, is that they went

01:01:36.170 --> 01:01:37.339
out and hunted for meat.

01:01:37.339 --> 01:01:40.230
And chimps like meat, they’ll eat it whenever
they can, although they’re not very good

01:01:40.230 --> 01:01:41.230
hunters.

01:01:41.230 --> 01:01:42.230
But they will definitely eat meat.

01:01:42.230 --> 01:01:44.749
And human beings are so good at hunting that
we probably…

01:01:44.749 --> 01:01:48.089
well, for example, we probably wiped out the
mammoths.

01:01:48.089 --> 01:01:52.670
And when human beings came into North America,
there were as many different kinds of large

01:01:52.670 --> 01:01:57.359
animal in North America as there were in Africa,
and human beings killed all of them.

01:01:57.359 --> 01:02:03.210
And that was just with… you know, like they
didn’t have our notion of technologically

01:02:03.210 --> 01:02:07.220
sophisticated weapons, it was bows and arrows
and clubs and spears.

01:02:07.220 --> 01:02:12.180
Nonetheless, they got rid of everything, you
know.

01:02:12.180 --> 01:02:20.270
Large cats, giant beavers, mammoths… we’re
very, very, very, very dangerous.

01:02:20.270 --> 01:02:29.049
Anyways, there’s another archetype, I would
say it’s a maternal archetype in a sense,

01:02:29.049 --> 01:02:32.130
and one of the things that’s very interesting
about human males is that they’re quite

01:02:32.130 --> 01:02:35.769
maternal, by comparison to other animals.

01:02:35.769 --> 01:02:43.150
So for example, the mother bear doesn’t
have the male bear, the boar… is it boars

01:02:43.150 --> 01:02:44.450
and sows with bears?

01:02:44.450 --> 01:02:45.740
I think it is.

01:02:45.740 --> 01:02:48.599
Anyways, you keep the cub away from the male,
because he’ll kill them.

01:02:48.599 --> 01:02:57.359
Now, not all males will do that but many will,
they don’t have any filial attachment.

01:02:57.359 --> 01:03:02.440
And that’s the case for many but not all
complex mammals.

01:03:02.440 --> 01:03:13.160
Pair bonding on a permanent basis is relatively
rare, but joint provision of children, that’s

01:03:13.160 --> 01:03:14.700
exceedingly rare.

01:03:14.700 --> 01:03:17.079
And men are pretty good at taking care of
children.

01:03:17.079 --> 01:03:20.000
I would say they’re not as good at taking
care of infants as women are, because they

01:03:20.000 --> 01:03:23.740
don’t have the full range of resources at
hand.

01:03:23.740 --> 01:03:31.119
And they’re not, I don’t think they have
the same kind of immediate intuitive understanding

01:03:31.119 --> 01:03:34.989
of, or attraction to, babies that women have.

01:03:34.989 --> 01:03:40.210
I think that switch is around the age of two
or two and a half, when the kids can engage

01:03:40.210 --> 01:03:42.190
in rough-and-tumble play.

01:03:42.190 --> 01:03:46.880
Because men are much more likely to engage
in rough-and-tumble play than women are, and

01:03:46.880 --> 01:03:51.359
rough-and-tumble play seems to be one of the
things that civilises children.

01:03:51.359 --> 01:03:54.869
And it’s partly because, you know one of
the things you have to learn when you’re

01:03:54.869 --> 01:04:00.339
a child is what actually hurts you and what
can be ignored, and what actually hurts someone

01:04:00.339 --> 01:04:02.130
else and what can be ignored.

01:04:02.130 --> 01:04:04.470
And the best way to learn that is in rough-and-tumble
play.

01:04:04.470 --> 01:04:08.599
Because what happens is that if you’re playing
with a kid, they get more and more excited

01:04:08.599 --> 01:04:12.599
and they do more and more crazy things, until
they do something that either hurts them or

01:04:12.599 --> 01:04:16.460
hurts you, and then you have to say, look,
you can’t do that.

01:04:16.460 --> 01:04:20.769
And it’s like a dance in a sense, you know,
when you’re playing with a kid.

01:04:20.769 --> 01:04:25.249
And you have to let them win fairly frequently,
because otherwise they don’t continue the

01:04:25.249 --> 01:04:26.249
game.

01:04:26.249 --> 01:04:29.700
And you can tell that because if the game
is going well then they’re laughing with

01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:34.299
insane life, because kids just love rough-and-tumble
play.

01:04:34.299 --> 01:04:39.260
And they love crazy things and catch them
and they think that’s just great.

01:04:39.260 --> 01:04:42.920
But partly what you’re doing is you’re
extending their body in all sorts of ways

01:04:42.920 --> 01:04:48.839
and getting them to learn, in an embodied
sense, where their limits are and where the

01:04:48.839 --> 01:04:49.999
limits of other people are.

01:04:49.999 --> 01:04:50.999
And that’s embodied.

01:04:50.999 --> 01:04:55.400
And children who haven’t had that, man they’re
just so awkward, it’s unbelievable.

01:04:55.400 --> 01:04:59.420
You know, they might have some hypothetical
sense of how to interact with another person,

01:04:59.420 --> 01:05:03.230
but they don’t have an embodied sense of
it, and they can’t tell the difference between

01:05:03.230 --> 01:05:04.750
what hurts them and what doesn’t.

01:05:04.750 --> 01:05:09.410
Because often a young child, if they’re
startled, they’ll start to cry.

01:05:09.410 --> 01:05:13.240
And then you can say, well that didn’t hurt,
and then they’ll notice, well yeah it didn’t

01:05:13.240 --> 01:05:14.630
hurt, and they’ll stop crying.

01:05:14.630 --> 01:05:20.319
But if they haven’t had their limits tested,
they’ll cry at anything unexpected, even

01:05:20.319 --> 01:05:21.319
if it doesn’t hurt.

01:05:21.319 --> 01:05:26.009
And that’s not good, because if you cry
under all conditions the probability that

01:05:26.009 --> 01:05:28.420
you’ll make friends when you’re three
is zero.

01:05:28.420 --> 01:05:31.950
Because another kid will start playing with
you and maybe playing rough, and if you burst

01:05:31.950 --> 01:05:36.749
into tears, you know, several times, the kid’s
just going to go somewhere else and find a

01:05:36.749 --> 01:05:37.749
different playmate.

01:05:37.749 --> 01:05:39.519
You know, they’ll say you’re just a baby.

01:05:39.519 --> 01:05:42.170
Which is roughly, exactly accurate…

01:05:42.170 --> 01:05:47.400
roughly exactly [laughter]… roughly accurate!

01:05:47.400 --> 01:05:52.420
Anyways, back to the archetypal representation.

01:05:52.420 --> 01:05:58.160
I think really the way to think about it is
that for men, the hero archetype is the archetype

01:05:58.160 --> 01:06:03.430
that’s dominant and in the forefront, and
the maternal archetype is subordinate and

01:06:03.430 --> 01:06:06.309
in the background, I mean inside their own
psyches.

01:06:06.309 --> 01:06:08.700
Whereas with women it’s reversed.

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:13.420
So each of the genders can play the role of
the other gender but there’s a tilt in each

01:06:13.420 --> 01:06:17.809
of them towards the… what would you say?

01:06:17.809 --> 01:06:24.640
Well I would say towards typical gender-normative
behaviour.

01:06:24.640 --> 01:06:28.500
Now the social constructivists believe that
no such thing exists, but those people are

01:06:28.500 --> 01:06:32.650
so pathological that even considering what
they have to say is a mistake.

01:06:32.650 --> 01:06:39.589
So, you know they act as if there is no biology
and everything’s cultural, and it’s like,

01:06:39.589 --> 01:06:42.089
well no, that’s just not right.

01:06:42.089 --> 01:06:46.931
Now what biology means in practice and what
you should do about it, that’s a whole different

01:06:46.931 --> 01:06:51.299
question, but to think of all these differences
as socially… height differences between

01:06:51.299 --> 01:06:55.369
men and women are not socially constructed,
and they’re relevant.

01:06:55.369 --> 01:06:59.509
And the upper-body strength differences between
men and women are not socially constructed,

01:06:59.509 --> 01:07:02.269
and they’re relevant too!

01:07:02.269 --> 01:07:06.140
So one of the things I was wondering about,
maybe you guys can help me clarify this or

01:07:06.140 --> 01:07:10.579
tell me where I’m not thinking about this
properly, and if you think there’s any…

01:07:10.579 --> 01:07:14.299
if I’m missing something let me know.

01:07:14.299 --> 01:07:20.119
One of the things my daughter said, she watched
my son one day have a fight with his best

01:07:20.119 --> 01:07:21.119
friend.

01:07:21.119 --> 01:07:23.130
It was a physical fight, you know.

01:07:23.130 --> 01:07:26.310
His best friend did something that my best
friend didn’t regard as appropriate and

01:07:26.310 --> 01:07:29.089
so he hit him, and then there was a fight.

01:07:29.089 --> 01:07:32.249
And then three days later they were friends
again.

01:07:32.249 --> 01:07:37.569
And my daughter said to me that she was very
annoyed by that, which wasn’t the fight

01:07:37.569 --> 01:07:41.039
exactly, but the fact that they could have
a fight and then they were friends again three

01:07:41.039 --> 01:07:46.380
days later, because she said that that option
wasn’t available to women, right?

01:07:46.380 --> 01:07:50.400
And the option is, the option that the men
have is, “well if you get out of hand I’ll

01:07:50.400 --> 01:07:56.529
just sock you one”, and then we’ll have
established where the boundaries of behaviour

01:07:56.529 --> 01:07:57.529
begin and end.

01:07:57.529 --> 01:08:03.829
And I would say that in my experience, if
I’m talking to someone who’s self-confident

01:08:03.829 --> 01:08:08.859
and masculine and accomplished, there’s
always an undercurrent of potential violence.

01:08:08.859 --> 01:08:10.869
It’s an undercurrent.

01:08:10.869 --> 01:08:14.130
And that’s actually an undercurrent of respect,
which means that there’s things that we

01:08:14.130 --> 01:08:18.520
can do to each other in a civilised way, but
there are rules that if you break, all hell’s

01:08:18.520 --> 01:08:19.730
going to break loose.

01:08:19.730 --> 01:08:24.170
And one of the consequences of that is that
it doesn’t.

01:08:24.170 --> 01:08:30.230
Now the typical bullying pattern for women
in high school and junior high is reputation

01:08:30.230 --> 01:08:31.970
destruction.

01:08:31.970 --> 01:08:40.279
And I’m wondering, what are the implications
for the conduct of behaviour if there is no

01:08:40.279 --> 01:08:43.400
recourse to aggression to solve disputes?

01:08:43.400 --> 01:08:46.600
Because they don’t get solved.

01:08:46.600 --> 01:08:51.830
You know, I’ve tried to analyse what it
means to say ‘no’ to someone, because

01:08:51.830 --> 01:08:52.830
‘no’ means something.

01:08:52.830 --> 01:08:53.830
‘No’ means…

01:08:53.830 --> 01:08:59.150
well if you tell a child ‘no’, what you’re
basically telling the child, as far as I can

01:08:59.150 --> 01:09:04.020
tell, is if you continue doing that something
you won’t like will happen to you.

01:09:04.020 --> 01:09:09.130
And if you’re civilised and say no what
happens is you say no a couple of times and

01:09:09.130 --> 01:09:13.909
then instead of devaluing the word, which
is what you do if you just said it over and

01:09:13.909 --> 01:09:19.190
over a hundred times with no consequences,
you take action that’s non-verbal.

01:09:19.190 --> 01:09:23.319
And so one of the things you might do is remove
the child from the situation, or you might

01:09:23.319 --> 01:09:24.319
put them on the steps.

01:09:24.319 --> 01:09:27.569
Or maybe you put them on the steps and they
run away, so you have to hold them on the

01:09:27.569 --> 01:09:28.569
steps.

01:09:28.569 --> 01:09:33.859
But the point is, the point to the child is,
there are limits, and if you exceed them,

01:09:33.859 --> 01:09:36.759
you will be physically controlled.

01:09:36.759 --> 01:09:40.549
What if there’s no option for physical control?

01:09:40.549 --> 01:09:41.549
What happens?

01:09:41.549 --> 01:09:46.049
Well I don’t know the answer to that, I
don’t know what women do about that but

01:09:46.049 --> 01:09:49.719
I also don’t know what men do about that
in relationship to women.

01:09:49.719 --> 01:09:52.810
So I was watching something the other day,
it was this…

01:09:52.810 --> 01:09:59.900
I posted it on my Twitter account as an example
of animus possession.

01:09:59.900 --> 01:10:03.400
So a woman and a man were having a dispute
on the street.

01:10:03.400 --> 01:10:04.400
It was a political dispute.

01:10:04.400 --> 01:10:10.110
And this particular woman, the way she was
behaving towards this particular man, was

01:10:10.110 --> 01:10:15.460
such that had she been a man, and had he been
a man, he would have definitely hit her.

01:10:15.460 --> 01:10:19.739
Because she was right in his face, and she
was cursing at him and swearing and calling

01:10:19.739 --> 01:10:20.739
him an idiot.

01:10:20.739 --> 01:10:21.739
And really being hyper-aggressive.

01:10:21.739 --> 01:10:24.400
And of course, he couldn’t do anything about
it.

01:10:24.400 --> 01:10:28.110
So then I was watching that and I was thinking,
just exactly what are you supposed to do in

01:10:28.110 --> 01:10:29.349
a situation like that?

01:10:29.349 --> 01:10:31.500
Because you’re screwed no matter what you
do.

01:10:31.500 --> 01:10:36.260
If you’re on the male side, it’s like,
you certainly can’t intervene physically.

01:10:36.260 --> 01:10:41.239
It’s like you’re dead in the water instantly,
instantly, in your eyes, the eyes of society,

01:10:41.239 --> 01:10:44.650
and in the woman’s eyes, instantly, if you
move past the boundary.

01:10:44.650 --> 01:10:50.369
If you leave, well, there’s no glory in
running away, that’s for sure.

01:10:50.369 --> 01:10:53.770
So what exactly is supposed to happen?

01:10:53.770 --> 01:10:57.770
I have no idea what’s supposed to happen
in a situation like that.

01:10:57.770 --> 01:11:01.489
I think what happens, what will happen over
time, is that men just won’t put themselves

01:11:01.489 --> 01:11:02.489
in those situations.

01:11:02.489 --> 01:11:09.030
I think what the male would have to do in
that situation is behave like a woman would,

01:11:09.030 --> 01:11:15.599
in a female dominance hierarchy.

01:11:15.599 --> 01:11:20.000
So he stands there and takes it and afterwards
goes off and bad-mouths her to her social

01:11:20.000 --> 01:11:21.000
circle.

01:11:21.000 --> 01:11:22.000
That’s a form of aggression.

01:11:22.000 --> 01:11:28.020
Right, right, but you know that’s definitely
not seen as an admirable form of behaviour

01:11:28.020 --> 01:11:29.770
among men.

01:11:29.770 --> 01:11:32.400
I mean, I don’t think it’s an admirable
form of behaviour, period.

01:11:32.400 --> 01:11:35.264
But I mean, you know, aggression is often
not admirable, so that’s fine.

01:11:35.264 --> 01:11:36.264
Yeah?

01:11:36.264 --> 01:11:37.264
I think that when you take away emotion, like
when someone’s acting really aggressively

01:11:37.264 --> 01:11:41.659
towards you and you reserve your emotion,
it gives them a lot less to feed off of, and

01:11:41.659 --> 01:11:55.000
that ends up taking power away in a similar
way that aggression does.

01:11:55.000 --> 01:11:59.280
Because people react aggressively and they
want feedback that gives them more excitement…

01:11:59.280 --> 01:12:00.280
Oh definitely.

01:12:00.280 --> 01:12:02.670
Oh yeah it would’ve been a real victory
for her if she would’ve gotten slapped.

01:12:02.670 --> 01:12:06.800
Absolutely, because it would prove everything
she said instantly.

01:12:06.800 --> 01:12:11.840
Yeah, but when you dismiss, power’s gone.

01:12:11.840 --> 01:12:13.420
So then you have all the power.

01:12:13.420 --> 01:12:14.420
Yeah, yeah.

01:12:14.420 --> 01:12:19.500
Well I think that that would be a reasonably
effective strategy for a street confrontation

01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:20.500
like that.

01:12:20.500 --> 01:12:25.820
I’m not so sure how well it would work in
a prolonged competition inside a company,

01:12:25.820 --> 01:12:26.920
for example.

01:12:26.920 --> 01:12:34.260
In a hierarchy, yeah, because you know the
truth of the matter is that men have no idea

01:12:34.260 --> 01:12:36.449
how to treat women if they’re in male power
hierarchies.

01:12:36.449 --> 01:12:39.219
They have no idea how to treat them.

01:12:39.219 --> 01:12:41.179
And that’s because we have no idea how to
do that.

01:12:41.179 --> 01:12:42.750
No one has any idea how to do that.

01:12:42.750 --> 01:12:52.860
It’s only been happening, in any pronounced
sense, for about, well, on a societal level,

01:12:52.860 --> 01:12:54.400
really probably since the mid-70s.

01:12:54.400 --> 01:12:56.300
You know, it’s three generations.

01:12:56.300 --> 01:12:58.579
It’s a drop in the bucket.

01:12:58.579 --> 01:13:01.510
And it’s a very, very difficult thing to
sort out.

01:13:01.510 --> 01:13:05.361
So, I mean part of the reason I’ve been
thinking about these sorts of things is because

01:13:05.361 --> 01:13:10.420
I’ve been very interested in the fact that
men have been doing very badly in junior high

01:13:10.420 --> 01:13:13.619
and high school, and they’re bailing out
of universities like mad.

01:13:13.619 --> 01:13:17.900
At the rate it’s going there’ll be almost
no men in most disciplines within ten years.

01:13:17.900 --> 01:13:20.989
I mean you can tell that even in this class.

01:13:20.989 --> 01:13:26.679
It’s funny because on my YouTube videos
I look at the gender distribution for viewing

01:13:26.679 --> 01:13:30.610
and it’s 80% male, which is interesting
to me.

01:13:30.610 --> 01:13:32.920
I’m not sure exactly why that is.

01:13:32.920 --> 01:13:39.820
But in lots of fields it’s 80:20 women already,
and I can’t see why that won’t…

01:13:39.820 --> 01:13:44.480
I think that’ll accelerate as we move forward,
rather than slowing down.

01:13:44.480 --> 01:13:52.780
Alright, so anyways, back to sexual selection
and the power hierarchy.

01:13:52.780 --> 01:14:01.360
Now from a Darwinian perspective, one of the
things you might do is call whatever selects

01:14:01.360 --> 01:14:04.199
‘nature’.

01:14:04.199 --> 01:14:09.020
And so when we think of nature and we think
about nature from a scientific perspective,

01:14:09.020 --> 01:14:13.719
we don’t even think about humans as agents
of nature, right?

01:14:13.719 --> 01:14:17.190
We tend to think of the human being as something
that stands against nature.

01:14:17.190 --> 01:14:18.389
That’s in mythology.

01:14:18.389 --> 01:14:24.150
It’s like, the human being against the natural
world, it’s a very common plot in movies

01:14:24.150 --> 01:14:25.719
and stories.

01:14:25.719 --> 01:14:28.790
So it’s man against nature, roughly speaking.

01:14:28.790 --> 01:14:33.239
So we tend to think of those as separate categories.

01:14:33.239 --> 01:14:42.630
But if women do the selection among human
beings, at least they do the intense selection,

01:14:42.630 --> 01:14:46.369
then women are nature, for men.

01:14:46.369 --> 01:14:48.530
And that’s exactly what the archetype is.

01:14:48.530 --> 01:14:50.410
It’s Mother Nature.

01:14:50.410 --> 01:14:56.159
And Mother Nature has a very vicious element,
and the vicious element of Mother Nature is

01:14:56.159 --> 01:14:57.510
rejection.

01:14:57.510 --> 01:15:01.400
And that can be maternal rejection, which
is, you know, certainly something that causes

01:15:01.400 --> 01:15:03.340
a tremendous amount of trauma.

01:15:03.340 --> 01:15:11.550
But it can also be the most fundamental form
of rejection, and the most fundamental form

01:15:11.550 --> 01:15:14.290
of rejection is sexual rejection.

01:15:14.290 --> 01:15:23.240
Because it means that you are judged as biologically
unworthy, roughly speaking.

01:15:23.240 --> 01:15:27.190
And there’s other reasons why… so the
reason I’m telling you this is because I’ve

01:15:27.190 --> 01:15:29.090
been trying to puzzle out…

01:15:29.090 --> 01:15:33.570
what you see in mythology quite clearly is
that the dominance hierarchy is represented

01:15:33.570 --> 01:15:36.760
as male, so culture is male, and nature is
female.

01:15:36.760 --> 01:15:38.650
Well, why the hell would nature be female?

01:15:38.650 --> 01:15:42.260
It’s like, okay, well I’ve given you one
reason, and I think it’s the primary reason.

01:15:42.260 --> 01:15:48.210
There’s other reasons, because nature is
also seen as that which produces natural forms,

01:15:48.210 --> 01:15:54.030
and of course, women produce natural forms,
so that’s a logical association.

01:15:54.030 --> 01:15:59.070
And then, what’s the other one?

01:15:59.070 --> 01:16:08.250
Well, we can leave it at that for the time
being.

01:16:08.250 --> 01:16:23.179
So, now let me show you how 

01:16:23.179 --> 01:16:24.920
I think that that’s represented.

01:16:24.920 --> 01:16:29.860
I think this is the way the world is structured
when you consider it as a place of action.

01:16:29.860 --> 01:16:35.420
So the world as a place of action has characters,
like the elements are characters, and the

01:16:35.420 --> 01:16:41.679
characters are engaged in dramatic behaviour.

01:16:41.679 --> 01:16:47.719
And there are classes of characters, and as
far as I can tell, this is a reasonably comprehensive

01:16:47.719 --> 01:16:49.790
classification of dramatic characters.

01:16:49.790 --> 01:16:54.420
We’ll start from the inside and go out.

01:16:54.420 --> 01:16:58.150
And you can think of any of these as primary,
by the way, and you’ll see that reflected

01:16:58.150 --> 01:16:59.560
in different kinds of mythology.

01:16:59.560 --> 01:17:05.030
So sometimes the maternal is primary, and
sometimes the culture is primary, and sometimes

01:17:05.030 --> 01:17:07.090
the hero, the individual is primary.

01:17:07.090 --> 01:17:12.550
And I think that’s because these three things
are irreducibly primary.

01:17:12.550 --> 01:17:17.860
In that, from the perspective of consciousness,
in that, to be conscious, there has to be

01:17:17.860 --> 01:17:23.060
you, but if you’re conscious you have to
have evolved within a culture and that’s

01:17:23.060 --> 01:17:28.520
culture is necessarily nested in the natural
world, and so wherever there’s one there’s

01:17:28.520 --> 01:17:29.900
all three.

01:17:29.900 --> 01:17:34.290
And the individual’s also the thing… without
the individual there wouldn’t be any separation

01:17:34.290 --> 01:17:35.710
between nature and culture.

01:17:35.710 --> 01:17:39.980
So you can see the individual consciousness
is the generator of the entire structure,

01:17:39.980 --> 01:17:42.310
but you can say that about any of the characters.

01:17:42.310 --> 01:17:48.810
So there’s the archetypal son, because the
hero tends to be masculine, and then, it has

01:17:48.810 --> 01:17:53.449
a… that character has a positive element
and a negative element.

01:17:53.449 --> 01:17:59.670
And the positive element is the good person,
and the negative element is the bad person.

01:17:59.670 --> 01:18:06.570
And I would say the archetypal representations
of those are, like, the saviour, and Satan.

01:18:06.570 --> 01:18:07.800
And that’s basically…

01:18:07.800 --> 01:18:13.070
there are portrayals like that in virtually
every system of mythology and religion.

01:18:13.070 --> 01:18:17.950
Now it seems to me that in some sense Christianity,
and this was probably under the influence

01:18:17.950 --> 01:18:27.360
of the Zoroastrians, developed the most articulated
representation of good vs. evil at the individual

01:18:27.360 --> 01:18:29.820
level.

01:18:29.820 --> 01:18:35.719
And that’s why you can identify two fundamental
characters in the Christian story, so to speak,

01:18:35.719 --> 01:18:36.760
that play out these roles.

01:18:36.760 --> 01:18:41.650
And they’re seen as eternal adversaries
locked in battle across time.

01:18:41.650 --> 01:18:45.280
And that would be, the battle takes place
in the natural world, the battle takes place

01:18:45.280 --> 01:18:50.560
in the cultural world, and the battle takes
place within, as well.

01:18:50.560 --> 01:18:54.091
And you can see that reflected in stories
all the time, because, you know, it’s so

01:18:54.091 --> 01:19:00.300
frequently the case that the hero, the super-hero
has his nemesis.

01:19:00.300 --> 01:19:05.739
And it’s the guy that he’s extraordinarily
well-matched with who continuously tests his

01:19:05.739 --> 01:19:09.440
wits and whose motivation is essentially destructive.

01:19:09.440 --> 01:19:13.340
So you see that with Batman and the Joker,
for example, or you see that with Sherlock

01:19:13.340 --> 01:19:15.969
Holmes and Moriarty.

01:19:15.969 --> 01:19:23.210
There’s always this pairing and that’s
partly because I think the archetypal realms

01:19:23.210 --> 01:19:27.720
of human decision-making are something like,
are you working to make things better or are

01:19:27.720 --> 01:19:29.320
you working to make things worse?

01:19:29.320 --> 01:19:33.619
Are you possessed by resentment and hatred,
so that you want things to degenerate and

01:19:33.619 --> 01:19:40.590
burn, or are you hopeful and capable of manifesting
faith in the future so that you’re working

01:19:40.590 --> 01:19:42.130
to make things better?

01:19:42.130 --> 01:19:46.800
And then there’s a battle in everyone, in
their own psyches, and then there’s a battle

01:19:46.800 --> 01:19:47.800
in culture.

01:19:47.800 --> 01:19:51.520
There’s a battle everywhere between those
two fundamental perspectives.

01:19:51.520 --> 01:19:57.429
And then the archetypal son is the son of
the Great Father, and the Great Father represents

01:19:57.429 --> 01:20:00.179
two things, order and tyranny.

01:20:00.179 --> 01:20:05.989
So there’s the hero and the adversary, at
the individual level, and at the social level

01:20:05.989 --> 01:20:07.590
there’s order and tyranny.

01:20:07.590 --> 01:20:13.150
And so, roughly speaking, that’s either
the wise king or the despot.

01:20:13.150 --> 01:20:17.020
And the despot is someone who tries to eat
his own sons, which is actually a fairly common

01:20:17.020 --> 01:20:18.790
mythological trope.

01:20:18.790 --> 01:20:23.540
And you know, you’re under the thumb of
the despot when you’re not allowed, really,

01:20:23.540 --> 01:20:26.060
either to be hero or adversary.

01:20:26.060 --> 01:20:32.790
You’re just a cog in the structure of the
social world.

01:20:32.790 --> 01:20:34.280
So that’s despotism.

01:20:34.280 --> 01:20:40.369
On the orderly side, well, culture is what
we’re sitting in right now.

01:20:40.369 --> 01:20:43.679
I mean, this is social order that encapsulates
us.

01:20:43.679 --> 01:20:44.760
Everything about it is social order.

01:20:44.760 --> 01:20:50.719
I mean, there are rows, they’re roughly
linear, you know everyone’s in their place,

01:20:50.719 --> 01:20:56.260
everyone knows what to do, we’re unbelievably
protected from virtually anything you could

01:20:56.260 --> 01:20:58.199
possibly imagine.

01:20:58.199 --> 01:21:03.790
And so you know, we’re in our father’s
living room, so to speak.

01:21:03.790 --> 01:21:11.130
And in that living room, it’s a safe space…
oh God I should never have said that… [laughter]

01:21:11.130 --> 01:21:17.280
anyways, it’s a well-protected space in
which people are able to play and explore.

01:21:17.280 --> 01:21:24.500
And it seems to me that one of the functions
that fathers play in a family, if they’re

01:21:24.500 --> 01:21:28.220
fulfilling their function properly, is that
what they do is they put a perimeter around

01:21:28.220 --> 01:21:33.550
the territory, so that inside the walls, so
to speak, it’s safe, and inside the safe

01:21:33.550 --> 01:21:35.530
walls the children can play and explore.

01:21:35.530 --> 01:21:38.900
And in their playing and exploring they develop
their own individuality.

01:21:38.900 --> 01:21:43.090
And so, you know, you keep the darkness at
bay, and you can certainly see that that’s

01:21:43.090 --> 01:21:46.810
what the children want, because as soon as
you put them in a dark room, the first thing

01:21:46.810 --> 01:21:52.490
they say is, you know, come chase the monsters
away out from underneath the bed.

01:21:52.490 --> 01:21:56.860
And you know they wouldn’t be thinking that
if they hadn’t had an extraordinarily long

01:21:56.860 --> 01:22:01.639
evolutionary history of being chased by monsters
in the dark.

01:22:01.639 --> 01:22:05.630
And there are monsters in the dark, and everyone
knows that.

01:22:05.630 --> 01:22:11.770
Which is why you tell your children not to
go play at night after it’s dark, even now.

01:22:11.770 --> 01:22:17.190
So order and tyranny are the representations
of culture.

01:22:17.190 --> 01:22:21.380
And the thing is that there’s a constant
interplay between those two because you can

01:22:21.380 --> 01:22:26.590
never tell exactly when order has gone too
far and become tyranny.

01:22:26.590 --> 01:22:32.679
Andy you can never tell really when there’s
so little order that, well the next thing

01:22:32.679 --> 01:22:35.710
happens which is the emergence of chaos.

01:22:35.710 --> 01:22:37.870
And chaos is the Great Mother.

01:22:37.870 --> 01:22:39.860
And it’s creative and destructive.

01:22:39.860 --> 01:22:44.940
And that’s nature itself.

01:22:44.940 --> 01:22:48.179
And then on the outside, the very outside,
and this is the most complicated category

01:22:48.179 --> 01:22:53.440
of all, is that, it’s the dragon of chaos,
and it represents something that’s very

01:22:53.440 --> 01:22:54.440
difficult to conceptualise.

01:22:54.440 --> 01:23:03.179
But it’s the un-configured substructure
of being that gives rise to these other categories.

01:23:03.179 --> 01:23:09.389
So Donald Rumsfeld at one point made a very
famous comment, it became famous because it

01:23:09.389 --> 01:23:10.610
fits an archetypal structure.

01:23:10.610 --> 01:23:14.440
He said that there are known unknowns and
unknown unknowns.

01:23:14.440 --> 01:23:18.719
Okay, the dragon of chaos is the unknown unknown.

01:23:18.719 --> 01:23:23.310
It’s so unknown that you don’t even know
it’s there, but it can raise its head at

01:23:23.310 --> 01:23:24.330
any given point.

01:23:24.330 --> 01:23:27.860
It’s the thing that leaps out of you in
the darkness.

01:23:27.860 --> 01:23:30.860
It’s like, is it Zika, is that the new virus?

01:23:30.860 --> 01:23:32.710
It’s like the Zika virus.

01:23:32.710 --> 01:23:34.420
It’s like, no one saw it coming.

01:23:34.420 --> 01:23:38.070
It’s a completely new manifestation of being.

01:23:38.070 --> 01:23:44.810
And so, known unknowns you can make plans
to circumvent, unknown unknowns, they’re

01:23:44.810 --> 01:23:45.810
completely unpredictable.

01:23:45.810 --> 01:23:50.190
And what’s so interesting, this is where
it’s so bloody complicated, because you

01:23:50.190 --> 01:23:59.650
really have to think about things differently
to grasp what this means.

01:23:59.650 --> 01:24:03.320
This is part of the reason why I had trouble
with this section of the course on alchemy,

01:24:03.320 --> 01:24:09.300
because it’s so complicated that it’s…
okay, so here, think of it this way.

01:24:09.300 --> 01:24:11.530
What is the unknown made of?

01:24:11.530 --> 01:24:15.300
That’s a pretty weird question.

01:24:15.300 --> 01:24:18.450
So I’ll try to walk you through it, and
we won’t get very far with this, because

01:24:18.450 --> 01:24:23.590
we’ll have to do it 15 or 20 times I think
before it starts to become something that

01:24:23.590 --> 01:24:25.219
you can grapple with.

01:24:25.219 --> 01:24:30.840
Alright, so it’s September 11, 2011 and
the Twin Towers have just fallen.

01:24:30.840 --> 01:24:34.880
Sorry, 2001, yes, 9/11.

01:24:34.880 --> 01:24:37.909
Alright, what’s emerged?

01:24:37.909 --> 01:24:39.929
What’s happened?

01:24:39.929 --> 01:24:46.840
Well, one answer to that is that the towers
fell, but it’s a very, very poor answer.

01:24:46.840 --> 01:24:52.869
Because that was an unknown unknown, I would
say, and it traumatised people.

01:24:52.869 --> 01:24:56.640
I mean everyone, even in Toronto, walked around
in shock for about two days after that.

01:24:56.640 --> 01:25:00.410
And the reason for that was that we didn't
know what fell.

01:25:00.410 --> 01:25:04.829
The buildings fell, but the thing is the buildings
aren’t isolated.

01:25:04.829 --> 01:25:09.141
Like, they’re not some abandoned buildings
standing out there in the middle of a field

01:25:09.141 --> 01:25:13.310
in a barren wasteland in the middle of an
abandoned country.

01:25:13.310 --> 01:25:15.090
They weren’t buildings, exactly.

01:25:15.090 --> 01:25:17.820
They were only buildings at a superficial
level.

01:25:17.820 --> 01:25:23.440
First of all they had 5000 people in them,
or 5000 people died, if I remember correctly.

01:25:23.440 --> 01:25:28.750
And then all those people were networked out
with all sorts of other people.

01:25:28.750 --> 01:25:30.340
And they were networked at multiple levels.

01:25:30.340 --> 01:25:35.900
So they were family members but they were
also elements of the financial and economic

01:25:35.900 --> 01:25:36.900
machine.

01:25:36.900 --> 01:25:40.300
And in the political machine.

01:25:40.300 --> 01:25:46.130
And then what was hit was of course, not just
the buildings, but the economic and political

01:25:46.130 --> 01:25:48.520
system, whatever that means.

01:25:48.520 --> 01:25:54.329
And of course the people who took the towers
down weren’t trying to knock buildings down,

01:25:54.329 --> 01:25:59.639
which is what you saw happen, they were trying
to knock down the things the buildings invisibly

01:25:59.639 --> 01:26:01.179
related to.

01:26:01.179 --> 01:26:04.860
And then you might ask well, what were those
buildings invisibly related to?

01:26:04.860 --> 01:26:08.650
And it’s a simpler question to actually
reverse the question and ask, well what weren’t

01:26:08.650 --> 01:26:10.310
they related to?

01:26:10.310 --> 01:26:14.619
And the reason that people were traumatised
in the aftermath was because when we, when

01:26:14.619 --> 01:26:21.810
our bodies, in some sense, answered that question,
the answer was, uh oh, those buildings were

01:26:21.810 --> 01:26:25.679
probably attached to everything in every possible
way.

01:26:25.679 --> 01:26:29.810
And so God only knows what’s going to come
leaping out of that.

01:26:29.810 --> 01:26:37.040
So what happens when you see the buildings
fall, the facts that you see the buildings

01:26:37.040 --> 01:26:39.770
fall actually blinds you to what’s happening.

01:26:39.770 --> 01:26:43.690
It’s a very peripheral representation of
the actual event.

01:26:43.690 --> 01:26:48.030
Because you could also say, well it was a
psychological event, you know, it echoed inside

01:26:48.030 --> 01:26:49.030
of you.

01:26:49.030 --> 01:26:52.230
Well, you don’t see that, you feel it, and
you don’t really know what it means.

01:26:52.230 --> 01:26:58.240
But it puts a wave of uncertainty, a radical
wave of uncertainty, into everything.

01:26:58.240 --> 01:27:01.270
And not only the present, but also the future
for sure.

01:27:01.270 --> 01:27:05.130
Because things took a vicious turn after that,
and almost immediately, right?

01:27:05.130 --> 01:27:09.960
The whole political system readjusted itself,
it became much more authoritarian.

01:27:09.960 --> 01:27:12.520
You know, airports became little fascist enclaves.

01:27:12.520 --> 01:27:16.560
You know for me, for many people, I could
hardly go into an airport.

01:27:16.560 --> 01:27:23.850
It just drove me crazy to be lined up like
that and then subject to search, you know,

01:27:23.850 --> 01:27:30.000
by faceless representations of the paranoid
state.

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:33.050
God, that’s a horrible thing to have to
encounter.

01:27:33.050 --> 01:27:36.550
It’s a bloody horrible thing to train people
to get accustomed to.

01:27:36.550 --> 01:27:39.329
It’s really dangerous to train people to
accept that sort of thing.

01:27:39.329 --> 01:27:44.679
It was a way worse consequence I think, than
the buildings falling down themselves.

01:27:44.679 --> 01:27:46.880
But that was one thing that happened.

01:27:46.880 --> 01:27:47.880
What else happened?

01:27:47.880 --> 01:27:55.520
Well the one trillion dollar surplus that
was projected by the Clintons at the end of

01:27:55.520 --> 01:28:00.510
Bill Clinton’s presidency, which was the
prediction for the next ten years, that the

01:28:00.510 --> 01:28:07.780
economy was doing so well that the US government
was going to have a one trillion surplus over

01:28:07.780 --> 01:28:12.670
the next ten years, turned into a four trillion
dollar deficit.

01:28:12.670 --> 01:28:14.900
So that happened.

01:28:14.900 --> 01:28:20.421
Well and then we got tangled up even worse
in the Middle East, and of course that, I

01:28:20.421 --> 01:28:23.449
think that’s probably got worse as time’s
gone on rather than better.

01:28:23.449 --> 01:28:26.120
And we have no idea where that’s going to
end.

01:28:26.120 --> 01:28:30.179
You know, I mean Russia’s involved now,
and Russia rattled nuclear weapons at Turkey

01:28:30.179 --> 01:28:31.179
the other day.

01:28:31.179 --> 01:28:36.090
You know, even though Turkey’s their largest
trading partner and a member of NATO.

01:28:36.090 --> 01:28:40.410
And then you know, a million refugees flooded
into Europe, and God only knows what’s going

01:28:40.410 --> 01:28:43.530
to happen as a consequence of that.

01:28:43.530 --> 01:28:46.909
And it’s not like the people who planned
the bomb didn’t know that, like here’s

01:28:46.909 --> 01:28:48.080
what they thought.

01:28:48.080 --> 01:28:53.489
They thought “oh, the Soviet Union, that
was the second greatest power on Earth, and

01:28:53.489 --> 01:28:55.880
then they waded into Afghanistan.

01:28:55.880 --> 01:28:58.230
And they got tangled up in a terrorist war
and oh, poof!

01:28:58.230 --> 01:29:00.770
Ten years later they didn’t exist.

01:29:00.770 --> 01:29:03.040
Maybe we could do the same thing to the United
States”.

01:29:03.040 --> 01:29:06.440
It’s like, well, who knows?

01:29:06.440 --> 01:29:07.889
Maybe you could.

01:29:07.889 --> 01:29:10.770
I mean they certainly…

01:29:10.770 --> 01:29:16.579
the response to the 9/11 disaster, in my opinion,
was much more expensive than the disaster

01:29:16.579 --> 01:29:17.579
itself.

01:29:17.579 --> 01:29:24.760
I mean, one of the things that’s quite horrifying
is that here’s one statistic: nobody flew

01:29:24.760 --> 01:29:27.430
in the aftermath of 9/11 for a while.

01:29:27.430 --> 01:29:31.850
Like airplane flight bookings were way down.

01:29:31.850 --> 01:29:36.700
More people died in car accidents (because
of not flying) than were killed in the bombings.

01:29:36.700 --> 01:29:40.490
And no one ever noticed that, because people
are being killed on the highway all the time,

01:29:40.490 --> 01:29:41.490
right?

01:29:41.490 --> 01:29:43.179
I mean it’s background noise as far as we’re
concerned.

01:29:43.179 --> 01:29:49.340
But you know, it’s a really good example
of how not only do you not know what the event

01:29:49.340 --> 01:29:53.300
is, you can’t even tell who the event killed.

01:29:53.300 --> 01:29:58.599
And so it’s your apprehension of that, when
it occurs, that paralyses you.

01:29:58.599 --> 01:30:01.280
And so that’s the dragon of chaos, roughly
speaking.

01:30:01.280 --> 01:30:02.579
That’s an unknown unknown.

01:30:02.579 --> 01:30:08.719
It’s like all of reality, all of the multiple
levels of reality that exist around you are

01:30:08.719 --> 01:30:13.170
all of a sudden altered in an incomprehensible
manner.

01:30:13.170 --> 01:30:18.390
And then you could say that well, what happened
was the alteration of that reality.

01:30:18.390 --> 01:30:19.969
This is what happened.

01:30:19.969 --> 01:30:25.829
It’s that you can only perceive it, essentially,
as the explosion in the building and the consequent

01:30:25.829 --> 01:30:26.829
collapse.

01:30:26.829 --> 01:30:30.739
And so you might say that the Twin Towers
were brought down.

01:30:30.739 --> 01:30:36.150
It’s like that’s such a low-resolution
representation of what happened that it barely

01:30:36.150 --> 01:30:40.900
begins to describe that event.

01:30:40.900 --> 01:30:46.800
And so it’s that invisible background of
complex interrelationships, like a very, very

01:30:46.800 --> 01:30:49.560
complex melody, that’s always going on around
you.

01:30:49.560 --> 01:30:51.660
And as long as it works you don’t even notice
it.

01:30:51.660 --> 01:30:57.920
What happens is that it manifests itself as
predictable and desirable order.

01:30:57.920 --> 01:31:02.510
Which you can understand, and which is the
reason we have predictable and desirable order.

01:31:02.510 --> 01:31:06.810
This room is configured so that you can walk
into it without being afraid.

01:31:06.810 --> 01:31:10.520
And what that means is that it’s a radically
simplified version of reality.

01:31:10.520 --> 01:31:15.750
You know, and you can see that, I mean, it’s
even got padded walls, and that’s to stop

01:31:15.750 --> 01:31:18.310
the sound bouncing so that it won’t annoy
you.

01:31:18.310 --> 01:31:23.550
You know I mean it’s warm, it’s comfortable,
it’s sort of neutral so it fades into the

01:31:23.550 --> 01:31:24.650
background.

01:31:24.650 --> 01:31:27.750
Everyone here is civilised beyond belief.

01:31:27.750 --> 01:31:30.719
You hear about the radical individualism of
the West…

01:31:30.719 --> 01:31:34.520
I think of all the nonsense I’ve ever heard
that’s got to be the most nonsensical, we’re

01:31:34.520 --> 01:31:37.980
so bloody obedient that it’s insane.

01:31:37.980 --> 01:31:43.219
If you go to Edmonton at 3 o’clock in the
morning and watch pedestrians, they will stop

01:31:43.219 --> 01:31:47.730
at the ‘don’t walk’ light and wait until
it turns green [laughter].

01:31:47.730 --> 01:31:52.310
Now the roads are wide and so they… but
it’s still the case.

01:31:52.310 --> 01:31:54.060
It’s like, really??

01:31:54.060 --> 01:31:55.880
You think you’re an individual, really?

01:31:55.880 --> 01:31:59.390
And I’m not complaining about it, I think
it’s an amazing thing because you know we

01:31:59.390 --> 01:32:03.110
can really zip cars through our cities because
people don’t run out into the middle of

01:32:03.110 --> 01:32:07.210
the streets and get killed, they stay where
they’re supposed to so it’s incredibly

01:32:07.210 --> 01:32:08.280
efficient.

01:32:08.280 --> 01:32:14.530
But we’re so obedient and well-behaved that
it’s beyond… if you think about us as

01:32:14.530 --> 01:32:18.719
chimpanzees with clothes, you know, we’re
doing pretty damn well.

01:32:18.719 --> 01:32:22.909
And we set up our environments so that they’re
so simplified that we never have to encounter

01:32:22.909 --> 01:32:32.020
this multi-dimensional layered reality of
patterns that’s just moving around us all

01:32:32.020 --> 01:32:34.130
the time and that actually makes up reality.

01:32:34.130 --> 01:32:37.949
That’s reality, that’s what the world
is made out of.

01:32:37.949 --> 01:32:41.900
That’s a way different way of thinking about
it than to think of the world as made up of,

01:32:41.900 --> 01:32:42.920
say, of atoms.

01:32:42.920 --> 01:32:49.099
Because you think, that way you just think,
well it’s marbles in space, you know.

01:32:49.099 --> 01:32:53.360
And of course atoms aren’t marbles, and
everyone knows that, like God only knows what

01:32:53.360 --> 01:32:54.360
they are.

01:32:54.360 --> 01:32:57.140
But that’s still the image, that’s still
the metaphor, of reality.

01:32:57.140 --> 01:32:58.980
But that’s not what reality is.

01:32:58.980 --> 01:33:07.429
It’s an insanely patterned orchestra of
phenomena and that’s really what it is and

01:33:07.429 --> 01:33:12.550
then what happens is that you’re in this
little orderly sub-compartment in the middle.

01:33:12.550 --> 01:33:14.969
It’s like, you’re in a box and the box
is in a box.

01:33:14.969 --> 01:33:16.020
Well, let’s think about it.

01:33:16.020 --> 01:33:20.340
You’re in a box, okay, this box is in a
building, that’s a box.

01:33:20.340 --> 01:33:22.639
The building is in the university, that’s
a box.

01:33:22.639 --> 01:33:24.840
The university is in the city, that’s a
box.

01:33:24.840 --> 01:33:27.790
The city is in the province, that’s a box.

01:33:27.790 --> 01:33:32.409
Then it’s in Canada, then it’s covered
by the American nuclear umbrella, you know,

01:33:32.409 --> 01:33:37.210
and then it’s embedded in this immense history
of individual rights and freedoms that have

01:33:37.210 --> 01:33:41.199
manifested themselves as part of the political
and economic order.

01:33:41.199 --> 01:33:46.219
And all of that’s going on around you and
people are working like mad, crazily, to keep

01:33:46.219 --> 01:33:52.050
all of those boxes intact, and here you are
sitting and thinking, well, what you see around

01:33:52.050 --> 01:33:53.050
you is reality.

01:33:53.050 --> 01:33:58.690
It’s like it is, yeah, but man, there’s
a lot of people making this reality so simple

01:33:58.690 --> 01:34:00.889
that you don’t have to think about it.

01:34:00.889 --> 01:34:06.840
You can come in here and think about ideas,
you know for like hours at a time, and you

01:34:06.840 --> 01:34:09.040
don’t get eaten by a crocodile.

01:34:09.040 --> 01:34:13.480
It’s really… and you know no one comes
running through here pillaging, which is of

01:34:13.480 --> 01:34:14.500
course…

01:34:14.500 --> 01:34:19.150
the crocodile eating and the pillaging were
very, very common occurrences in our evolutionary

01:34:19.150 --> 01:34:20.670
history.

01:34:20.670 --> 01:34:25.050
For lots of our ancestors going down to get
water from the local river was a life or death

01:34:25.050 --> 01:34:26.050
mission.

01:34:26.050 --> 01:34:31.280
I mean I can’t imagine having to get water
from a crocodile infested stream.

01:34:31.280 --> 01:34:35.829
Especially when that’s where the lions are
hanging out too, you know.

01:34:35.829 --> 01:34:37.610
So, that’s order.

01:34:37.610 --> 01:34:39.389
That’s the positive element of order.

01:34:39.389 --> 01:34:43.500
Now you can say, students say this a lot:
“oh my God, I’m so constrained within

01:34:43.500 --> 01:34:47.110
these boxes, it’s destroying my individuality”
you know.

01:34:47.110 --> 01:34:48.190
And that’s the tyrannical…

01:34:48.190 --> 01:34:54.989
I mean it’s pathetic in some sense, because
God, you’re so privileged it’s beyond

01:34:54.989 --> 01:34:56.090
comprehension.

01:34:56.090 --> 01:35:02.469
But having said that, it is also the case,
and the case has to be made, that you do have

01:35:02.469 --> 01:35:06.690
to sacrifice your potential to cultural norms.

01:35:06.690 --> 01:35:10.610
And that is… you can argue that that’s
destructive.

01:35:10.610 --> 01:35:12.099
And sometimes it is destructive.

01:35:12.099 --> 01:35:15.100
Like creative people don’t fit very well
into nested boxes.

01:35:15.100 --> 01:35:19.469
And in order for them to fit they have to
do damage in some sense to their psyches,

01:35:19.469 --> 01:35:28.219
because they’re not naturally made to…
that isn’t their natural environment, boxes

01:35:28.219 --> 01:35:29.580
within boxes.

01:35:29.580 --> 01:35:31.320
And so they might say, well I’m being oppressed.

01:35:31.320 --> 01:35:34.080
It’s like, well of course you’re being
oppressed.

01:35:34.080 --> 01:35:38.619
If you’re in fifty boxes that’s constrain
your freedom of movement, but maybe you should

01:35:38.619 --> 01:35:41.790
be somewhat grateful for the fact that the
boxes exist.

01:35:41.790 --> 01:35:43.630
You think, well you don’t even notice them.

01:35:43.630 --> 01:35:47.420
The boxes are so effective that you don’t
even notice them.

01:35:47.420 --> 01:35:52.290
You can even criticise them, and you should,
you know, maybe they could be better and not

01:35:52.290 --> 01:35:54.429
quite as rigid, and so forth.

01:35:54.429 --> 01:36:03.780
And the criticism is justified but you have
to keep both of the representations in mind

01:36:03.780 --> 01:36:05.600
at the same time, you know.

01:36:05.600 --> 01:36:08.599
Your culture is the wise king and the despot
at the same time.

01:36:08.599 --> 01:36:16.390
And people hate that sort of thing because…
and you know our rational minds and our history

01:36:16.390 --> 01:36:22.179
of logical intellectual thinking has forced
us to assume that a thing can’t be itself

01:36:22.179 --> 01:36:24.619
and its opposite at the same time.

01:36:24.619 --> 01:36:30.309
But unfortunately when you’re talking about
very large-scale categories, things are what

01:36:30.309 --> 01:36:32.800
they are and their opposite at the same time.

01:36:32.800 --> 01:36:38.449
Because culture is the wise king and the tyrant,
and that means it’s very difficult to adjust

01:36:38.449 --> 01:36:42.150
your behaviour in relationship to it, because
you never know if you’re talking to the

01:36:42.150 --> 01:36:43.320
wise king or the tyrant.

01:36:43.320 --> 01:36:45.930
You know, and you feel that.

01:36:45.930 --> 01:36:49.260
If you have to do your taxes or if you have
to deal with the university bureaucracy in

01:36:49.260 --> 01:36:53.829
some way, it’s like, you’re a number,
you know, and you’re just annoying, like

01:36:53.829 --> 01:36:57.500
patients in the hospital, because hospitals
would run a lot better if they didn’t have

01:36:57.500 --> 01:36:58.949
any patients in them, obviously.

01:36:58.949 --> 01:37:02.600
And you know, you feel that, of course, whenever
you’re in a very complex bureaucracy.

01:37:02.600 --> 01:37:06.400
It’s like, you’re exchangeable, and that’s
very annoying.

01:37:06.400 --> 01:37:08.660
But by the same token, here you are.

01:37:08.660 --> 01:37:13.599
So you have to criticise… this is also a
very complex form of moral behaviour.

01:37:13.599 --> 01:37:16.929
You have to criticise with gratitude.

01:37:16.929 --> 01:37:22.230
And that’s something that students should
really be taught in university, because the

01:37:22.230 --> 01:37:27.449
opposite of gratitude is resentment, and there
is nothing that’s more pathological than

01:37:27.449 --> 01:37:28.449
resentment.

01:37:28.449 --> 01:37:33.870
The only thing that comes close to resentment,
in terms of its capacity to produce misery,

01:37:33.870 --> 01:37:38.940
is arrogance, and those things are often…
arrogance, resentment, and deceit… it’s

01:37:38.940 --> 01:37:40.170
like that’s the evil triad.

01:37:40.170 --> 01:37:44.300
You get all those three things going in your
life and boy, you’re going to be in rough

01:37:44.300 --> 01:37:47.520
shape very, very rapidly.

01:37:47.520 --> 01:37:53.719
So then, let’s take a break, for fifteen
minutes, okay?

